invalid type: string "fFEDERAL WEALTH TRANSFER TAX OUTLINE Chapter 1 A Historical Review Introduction The changing character of revenue sources and the degree to which any particular source is drawn upon are part of our economic, political, and social history Initially, the first estate and inheritance taxes were passed as a result of war and the corresponding need to generate revenue At the beginning of the 20th century, there was a movement to enact the estate tax in response to growing concentrations of wealth created by the industrial revolution The rationale is that by requiring a decedent to part with wealth, it will prevent the accumulation of wealth and spread wealth through out all classes President T. Roosevelt stated: The desire to provide security for ones self and ones family is natural and wholesome, but it is adequately served by a reasonable inheritance. Great accumulations of wealth cannot be justified on the basis of personal and family security. In the last such analysis such accumulations amount to the perpetuation of great and undesirable concentrations of control in a relatively few individuals over the employment and welfare of many others Because of the difficulty of applying the estate tax to inter vivos transfers, and because Republicans wanted to reduce the surtax rates for the top income brackets, the gift tax entered our federal revenue system In 1976, the generation skipping tax was instituted. Under prior law, wealthy individuals could utilize trusts to skip transfer taxes on one or more intervening generations, even though the skipped generations derived substantial economic benefits from and exercised significant control over the trust In general, distributions from the principal of a trust that skipped a generation were taxed at the unified transfer tax rate that would have been applicable if the intervening generation had actually made the transfer However, the tax generally was to be paid only out of the trust property, not by the skipped generation The 2001 Act gradually increases the amount exempt from estate tax and generation skipping tax from $1 million in 2002 to $3.5 million in 2009, and reduction of the highest tax rate in 1% increments from 50% in 2002 to 45% in 2009 In 2010, the estate and generation skipping taxes are repealed See Problems for Chapter 1 Chapter 6 Data on US Wealth and Wealth Transfer Taxes Distribution of Wealth in the United States (CB pg. 38) Figure 3 (estimated share of net worth held by top 1% of US population) shows a marked decline in the concentration of asset holdings between 1922 and 1947 The estate tax estimates indicate no systematic trend in the share of wealth held by the top .5 and 1 percent from 1947 to 1982, but suggests an abrupt and sizable increase thereafter It is not known if the wealth transfer taxes have caused a decrease in the concentration of wealth or if the result was due to other factors such as increased population, more ability to become wealthy, or because of the intrahousehold equalization in wealth holding between spouses Data from the 1990s suggest that, while the nominal wealth of the nations top wealth holders increased, it did not do so at a rate any different that that for the general public Wealth Transfer Tax Data The percentage of decedents liable for the estate tax grew throughout the post war era reaching a peak in the mid 1970s The substantial revision to the estate tax in the mid 1970s and subsequent further modifications in 1981 reduced the percentage of decedents liable for the estate tax Since that time, the percentage of decedents liable for the estate tax has gradually increased Among developed countries, an inheritance tax is more common than the type of estate tax that is imposed in the US An inheritance tax generally is imposed upon the amount of wealth the transferee or donee receives rather than on the total wealth of the transferor See Chapter 6 problems Chapter 7 Policy Aspects of a Transfer Tax Introduction In choosing among the available forms of taxation, which are not exclusive, the government may seek to advance a number of goals Raise revenue, redistribute wealth, tax accumulated wealth, enhance progressivity, limit inheritance and increase equality of opportunity, and assist in the formation and efficient deployment of capital Some Particular Issues in the Present Transfer Tax System A normative tax transfer system is one that presumably will be regarded as equitable or fair by taxpayers Fairness or equity in the context of a normative transfer tax requires that persons transferring like amounts of wealth by gift or at death be subject to the same amount of transfer tax Provisions that depart from this standard are preferential The estate and gift tax systems are separate despite attempts to unify the two The failure to gross up lifetime transfers by the amount of the gift tax in effect gives the inter vivos donor a deduction for gift tax paid, but a similar deduction is not accorded death time transfers There is thus a preference for lifetime transfers normally Based on the 2001 Act, there is now a preference for transfers at death because the exclusion amount was raised for estate taxes but not gift taxes Some transfers are excluded from the tax base because of the type of property that is transferred of the nature of the recipient See Chapter 7 Problems Chapter 5 A Brief Summary of Transfer Tax Procedure Filing a Return and Payment of Tax Gift Tax Taxable gifts are calculated on an annual basis and a taxable gift results once transfers to any donee during the calendar year exceed the exemption amount Certain transfers in payment of tuition or medical expenses of a done, transfers in satisfaction of the transferors legal obligation to support another and transfers to a charity or spouse are excluded from the tax base Section 6019 requires any individual making a transfer by gift in any calendar year to file a gift tax return The return must be filed by 4/15 of the year following the calendar year in which the gifts were made 6075(b)(1) An extension of time for filing a return may be obtained, but unless the taxpayer is abroad, it is generally limited to 6 months 6081 The gift tax must be paid at the same time that the return is due 6151(a) The primary liability for the payment of the gift tax falls upon the donor 2505(c) If the tax is not paid by the donor when due, then the donee becomes personally liable to the extent of the value of the gift 6324(b) Estate Tax An estate tax return must be filed by the executor or administrator of the estate of every US citizen or resident the value of whose gross estate exceeds a threshold amount This threshold is designed to require the filing of an estate tax return unless the unified credit eliminates any possibility of estate tax liability The return must be filed not later than 9 months after the death of the decedent 6075(a), and if the executor is unable to file a complete return within the prescribed time, he may apply for an extension of time The return must be filed with the IRS Center for the state in which the decedent was domiciled at the time of death 6091(b)(3) Payment of the estate tax is due 9 months after the death of the decedent regardless of when the return is filed 6075, 6151(a) The estate tax is payable by the executor or administrator but, if there is no executor or administrator appointed, qualified, and acting within the US, the responsibility for payment of the tax shifts to any person in actual or constructive possession of any property of the decedent to the extent of the value of the property in their possession 6018(a) Generation Skipping Tax Direct Skip An inter vivos gift to a skip person, a person in a generation which is two or more generations below the generation of the transferor The tax is paid by the transferor Taxable Distribution Generally, a distribution from a trust to a skip person The tax is paid by the transferee Taxable Termination The termination of an interest held in trust which occurs by reason of death, lapse of time, release of power, or otherwise The tax is paid by the trustee In general, the return filing obligation is imposed on the person who is liable for payments of the tax Investigations of Returns and Settlement Procedures The Regional Service Center makes a preliminary inspection of returns for mathematical errors, and the Examination Division of the District Directors Office then audits the returns After an investigation and settlement discussions, if no agreement is reached, the District Director will either drop the case or send a 30 day letter to the taxpayer This letter contains the report of the examining agent and gives the taxpayer 30 days in which to choose one or three courses of action File a formal protest with the District Director and request a conference with the Appeals Office; Request a statutory deficiency notice, a 90 day letter from which an appeal may be taken to the Tax Court; or Conceded liability by executing the agreement form accompanying the 30 day letter, thereby closing the case If the taxpayer elects not to pursue the Appeals procedure, or if agreement cannot be reached with the Appeals Officer, a statutory deficiency notice will be issued On the issuance of this letter, the taxpayer has 90 days after the date of mailing in which to petition the Tax Court for review If this petition is not filed the tax must be paid and, if not paid, may be assessed and collected by appropriate procedures After payment, the taxpayer may still file a claim for refund and, if the claim is disallowed or not acted on within 6 months, bring suit in the District Court or Court of Federal Claims The Tax Court procedure permits the taxpayer to obtain judicial consideration of the case without first paying the deficiency claimed by the Commissioner Statute of Limitations, Interest, and Penalties The Commissioner has three years from the later of the due date of the return or the date the return was actually filed within which to assess a tax deficiency 6501(a) In the case of the estate tax, the three year period is extended to 6 years if the taxpayer omits from the gross estate items in excess of 25% of the gross estate stated in the return The period is unlimited if no return is filed or if the return is false or fraudulent with the intent to evade taxes The gift tax statute of limitations runs only if the gift is disclosed on a gift tax return in a manner adequate to apprise the Service of the nature of the gift Delinquent payments of tax and payments of deficiencies in tax bear interest at the under 6621 from the time the tax should have been paid The tax laws provide civil and criminal penalties, the former being treated as additions to tax and collected as part of the tax through the deficiency procedure Chapter 9 Basic Application of the Transfer Taxes General Description of Gift Tax (CB pg. 62) Sections 2501 and 2511 merely impose a tax upon the transfer of property by gift by any individual whether the transfer is in trust or otherwise, whether the gift is direct or indirect The gift tax is imposed at progressive rates upon all lifetime gifts made by individuals, resident or nonresident 2501(a), 2511(a) Only individuals make taxable gifts; nothing is said about transfers by corporations or other entities The tax is calculated on taxable gifts, defined as the total gifts during the calendar year minus allowable exclusions and deductions such as 1) the inflation adjusted annual exclusion 2503(b); 2) the unlimited exclusion for qualifying payments of tuition or medical expenses of another person 2503(e); 3) the charitable deduction 2055; and 4) the marital deduction 2056. Married couples may elect to treat gifts to third parties as though they have been made one half by each spouse To determine the tax for each calendar year for which a return is required, the donor follows three steps: Computes a tax at present rates (using 2001(c)) on the total taxable gifts made from June 6, 1932 to the end of the calendar year. 2502(a)(1) Subtracts a tax (computed at present rates) on the total of all taxable gifts made up to the beginning of the year. 2502(a)(2) The difference is the tentative tax for the current year Subtract the remaining unified transfer tax credit under 2505 The balance, if any, is the gift tax payable for the current year See examples pp. 63-64 The combination of graduated rates and computational cumulation of all lifetime gifts effectively subjects gifts in succeeding years to higher and higher gift tax rate brackets General Description of the Estate Tax The law starts out in 2001 by imposing a tax at progressive rates on the transfer at death of the taxable estate of every citizen or resident of the US The taxable estate, 2051 declares, is equal to the gross estate less certain allowable deductions Sections 2031 and 2033 indicate that the gross estate includes the value of all property interests owned by the decedent at the date of death. Such as bank accounts, real estate, and securities The executor then subtracts the deductions allowed by 2053 through 2056 from the value of the gross estate to determine the taxable estate These deductions include funeral and administrative expenses, claims against the estate, uncompensated casualty losses arising during settlement of the estate, bequests, marital deduction bequest, and state death taxes After the taxable estate has been determined, a tentative estate tax is computed by applying the rates set forth in 2001 to the sum of the taxable estate plus adjusted taxable gifts, and subtracting from the amount thus determined the gift tax payable on the decedents post 1976 gifts Sections 2010 through 2015 then allow five credits against the tax: The unified credit The credit for state death taxes The credit for foreign death taxes The credit for estate tax paid on prior transfers of property to the decedent, and The credit for gift taxes paid with respect to property transferred prior to 12/31/1976 and included in the gross estate See examples pp. 65-66 General Description of Generation Skipping Transfer Tax Generation skipping transfers are taxable distributions, taxable terminations, and direct skips. Each of these taxable events is defined be reference to four other defined terms: transferor, interest, skip person, and non-skip person Transferor The decedent and the transferor of an inter vivos transfer is the donor Interest A person has an interest in a trust if that person is currently eligible to receive or has a present right to received, income or corpus from the trust A future interest is not an interest in the trust for purposes of the generation skipping tax Skip Person One who is assigned to a generation that is two generations or more below that of the transferor A trust is a skip person if the only persons with an interest in the trust are skip persons or if no persons hold an interest in the trust and no distributions from the trust can be made to non-skip persons Non-skip Person Any person, including a trust, that is not a skip person Taxable Distribution A distribution, other than a taxable termination or a direct skip, from a trust to a skip person The tax is imposed on the beneficiary, and if paid by the trust, constitutes further taxable distribution Taxable Termination The termination of the interest of any beneficiary if thereafter any of the beneficiaries is a skip person and none of them is a non-skip person Thus, if a trust provides income to child A for life, remainder to grandchildren, the death of A is a taxable termination On the other hand, if the trust provided for income to be paid to children A and B until the death of the survivor, remainder the grandchildren, the death of A before B would not be a taxable termination, because at As death, a non-skip person, B, possess an interest in the trust The amount of a taxable termination is the value of the property in which the terminated interest existed, reduced by amounts that would have been deductible had the transfer been subject to the estate tax, and the tax is payable from the trusts assets Direct Skip Any transfer to a skip person that is subject to estate or gift tax when made by the transferor However, a transfer to or for the benefit of the transferors grandchildren after the grandchilds parents have died is not a direct skip The amount of a direct skip is the value of the property received by the transferee, and the tax is paid by the transferor, or in the case of a transfer from a trust, the trustee The amount of the generation skipping tax is the product of the taxable amount and the applicable rate Every individual is allowed an exemption to allocate among inter vivos and death time transfers The applicable rate is the maximum federal estate tax rate multiplied by an inclusion ratio of 1 minus the applicable fraction The applicable fraction is, in general, the ratio of the amount of the generation-skipping tax exemption allocated to the transfer over the value of the property transferred See example pp. 68-69 See Chapter 9 Problems Chapter 10 Coordinating the Computation of the Estate and Gift Taxes Attempts to Unify the Two Taxes Although formally separate, the gift and estate taxes prior to 2004 are in concept imposed at a single rate, prescribed by 2001(c), on all of an individuals wealth transfers, whether made during life or at death While 2505 provides a credit that may relieve the donor of current gift tax liability, the form of that relief a credit rather than a deduction or exclusion leaves the cumulative total of transfers unaffected for purposes of applying the 2001(c) rates to cumulative transfers beyond the amount sheltered by the credit A credit delivers the same benefit to all donors, regardless of the size of their gifts, by sheltering the lowest regions of the base from tax In 2004 however, the gift tax becomes decoupled from the estate tax The Relationship Between Lifetime Gifts and Estate Tax Liability Due to definitional differences in the estate and gift tax bases, certain transfers may be swept into the gross estate at death even if they were treated as completed lifetime gifts on which tax was paid Transfers of this kind, obviously, should be counted only once, and single taxation is accomplished by leaving them within the taxable estate but excluding them from adjustable taxable gifts In effect, the date of death value of such transfers is substituted for its date of gift value If after the gift, the property increases in value, the higher value will be reflected in the taxable estate on which the estate tax is imposed Subtraction of the gift tax previously paid on the transferors date of gift value ensures that this amount of tax is not doubled up Even if a gift is later included in the gross estate, transfer tax thus is imposed only once, at death, on the date of death value, with the gift tax is treated in effect, as a prepayment If a gift is later included in the gross estate but decreases in value after the gift, the lesser of date of death value is used in calculating the hypothetical estate tax Practically speaking, to the extent that the credit is used during life, it is unavailable at death Beginning in 2004 however, and continuing through 2009, a taxpayer may fully use her gift tax credit during life and have additional credit to use in her estate due to the increased estate tax credit available during those years A further incongruity is that the gift tax is tax exclusive while the estate tax is tax inclusive This means that the gift tax base does not include the amount of the gift tax itself, while the estate tax is imposed on the value of all property transferred at death, including the portion used to pay the estate tax The failure to gross up lifetime transfers by the amount of the gift tax in effect gives the inter vivos donor a deduction for the gift tax paid This perpetuates a tax preference for lifetime transfers; however, between 2004-2009 the preference shifts to date of death transfers See Chapter 10 Problems Chapter 8 Constitutional Aspects of Transfer Taxes Power to Impose Transfer Taxes Article I, Section 9 of the Constitution provides that a direct tax on property must be imposed in proportion to the census Knowlton v. Moore, held a federal inheritance tax constitutionally valid as an indirect tax The court reasoned that the inheritance tax was not a tax on property but was a tax on the passage or transmission of property by will or intestacy, similar to a duty or excise The Court found that lack of uniformity in state law relating to intestate succession did not translate into impermissible lack of uniformity of a federal statute that depends on state law in its operation The Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of the gift tax in Bromley v. McCaughan The court concluded that the gift tax was an indirect tax levied upon the use or exercise of power over property, not a direct tax upon general ownership of property Power to Apply Transfer Taxes Retroactively United States v. Carlton Retroactivity (14 months was ok) Issue: Does the amendment to 2057 apply retroactively to the transaction entered into by Carlton? As originally adopted in October 1986, 2057 permitted an estate tax deduction for one-half of the proceeds of any sale of employer securities by the executor of the estate to an employee stock ownership plan In December 1986, Carlton acting as executor of the will of Day purchased 1.5 million shares of MCI Communications for $11.2 million Two days later, he sold them to MCIs ESOP for $10.6 million and claimed a deduction for $5.3 million In December 1987, 2057 was amended to permit the deduction only where the securities sold to an ESOP were directly owned by the decedent immediately before death Because the amendment applied retroactively, as if it were incorporated in the original 1986 provision, the Service disallowed the deduction Holding: Because we conclude that retroactive application of the 1987 amendment to 2057 is rationally related to a legitimate legislative purpose, we conclude that the amendment as applied to Carltons transaction is consistent with the Due Process Clause. Verdict for IRS This Court repeatedly has upheld retroactive tax legislation against a due process challenge The due process standard to be applied to tax statutes with retroactive effect is the same as that generally applicable to retroactive economic legislation: Provided that the retroactive application of a statute is supported by a legitimate legislative purpose furthered by rational means, judgments about the wisdom of such legislation remain within the exclusive province of the legislative and executive branches Before the amendment, any estate purchase the stock in question to qualify for the deduction, and the result was that taxpayers were taking advantage of the law to the detriment of the treasury Therefore, Congress purpose in enacting the amendment was neither illegitimate or arbitrary Congress acted to correct what it reasonably viewed as a mistake in the original provision that would have created a significant and unanticipated revenue loss Second, Congress acted promptly and established only a modest a period of retroactivity The period in this instance was little over 1 year Nations Bank v. United States Retroactivity (8 months was ok) Issue: Did the enactment of OBRA apply retroactively to the estate of Ms. Garwood? In late 1992 Congress passed legislation to extend the 55% estate tax rate, but did not present it to President Bush within 10 days of adjournment and he used a pocket veto Thus the 55% rate lapsed and defaulted back to the 50% rate When Ms. Garwood died in March 1993, the applicable estate tax rate was 50% On 8/10/1993, President Clinton signed OBRA into law OBRA instituted the 55% rate and made the rate retroactive to include the estates of decedents who dies on after January 1 1993 Holding: The enactment of OBRA is to be applied retroactively to Ms. Garwoods estate The Constitution prohibits the enactment of ex posto facto law, this prohibition however, applies solely to criminal acts The taxpayer in this case has timely paid the tax and seeks a refund, thus, the taxpayer is not subject to any criminal penalty Under the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment a rational legislative purpose must justify a retroactive enactment The Supreme Court in Carlton, did not limit the retroactive application of revenue measures solely to enactments with a curative purpose In this case, OBRA promptly filed the temporal gap in the 55% rate Imposition of a uniform 55% rate on estate tax transfers regardless of the particular month in which they occur suffices to supply the requisite rational purpose Additionally, the period of retroactivity was only 8 months See Chapter 8 Problems CHAPTER 11: THE ROLE OF STATE LAW Commissioner v. Estate of Bosch Bosch Doctrine The Federal court is not bound by the determination of a lower state court determination of state property law. Decedent created revocable trust in favor of his wife, the instrument gave her a general power of appointment Later, wife executed an instrument purporting to release her general power of appointment and convert it into a special power In general, property subject to general power is includible in the estate under 2041 (which is why she did it) But, only general power is subject to the marital deduction Commissioner took the position that the marital deduction not allowed because it was not a general power Wifes estate took the position that the release (of general power) was ineffective, therefore, the deduction is allowed Estate brought action in tax court, which abstained pending state court judgment on whether there was a valid release State trial court held that there was no valid release, and appeals court affirmed ISSUE Whether the federal court is bound by the state courts determination HELD No REASONING First of all, the federal government was not a party to the state case, therefore no res judicata effect Second, the ruling was not from the highest state court (so the Rules of Decision Act N/A) Overall: State trial courts decisions should be given no regard Outcome of the highest state court must be followed (total regard) Decisions by intermediate courts should be considered unless the court is convinced that the highest state court would decide otherwise (due/proper regard). DISSENT (Douglas) This is not an issue of res judicata (It would not apply anyway because government was not party) Instead the issue is whether, absent fraud/collusion, a federal court can ignore a state court judgment when federal taxation depends upon property rights and when property rights rest on state law The result under the majority decision will be to tax the taxpayer for benefits which he does not have under state law Where a state court has reached a deliberate conclusion, where it has construed state law, the federal court should consider the decision to be an exposition of the controlling state law and give it effect as such DISSENT (Harlan) I would hold that in cases in which state adjudicated property rights are contended to have federal tax consequences, federal courts must attribute conclusiveness to the judgment of a state court, of whatever level in the state system, unless the litigation from which the judgment resulted does not bear the indicia of a genuinely adversary proceeding Estate of Goree v. Commissioner Robert died intestate, leaving W and 3 children Under state law, went to wife, and the remaining divided between the children Wife petitioned probate court to enter protective orders authorizing each child to execute partial disclaimers renouncing any interest in excess of $200,000 (equal to the $600,000 unified credit) Therefore the remaining money would go to wife (by operation of law), and qualify for the marital deduction Probate court held that disclaimer was in the best interests of the children, and therefore allowed it ISSUE Whether disclaimer was valid HELD Yes REASONING 2518(b) requires that the disclaimed interest pass without any direction from the disclaimant In other words, the disclaimer must result in a valid passing of the interest to such person by operation of the law Under state law (Alabama), it passes by law if in the best interests of the children The state court found that this was in the best interests, therefore, under state law, a reviewing court must affirm unless findings are plainly and palpably erroneous Overall: probate findings not plainly and palpably erroneous At this time, there was a $600,000 exemption Died intestate, and under state law, to surviving spouse, and to children equally Here, W went to post-mortum tax planner who advised her to have the children disclaim all but $600,000 (and by operation of state law, the rest goes to surviving spouse) The guardian ad litem determined that this was in the best interests of the children, but the IRS argues that it is not in their best interests Because it was only in their own interests because of the tax consequences As lawyer for the mother, you should advise her that the children should have independent counsel, because what if there is a family breakdown, and the kids get nothing Chapter 12 - The Scope of the Estate Tax: 2033 Beneficial Ownership (CB pg. 98) Sec. 2031. Definition of gross estate (a) General - The value of the gross estate of the decedent shall be determined by including to the extent provided for in this part, the value at the time of his death of all property, real or personal, tangible or intangible, wherever situated. Sec. 2033. Property in which the decedent had an interest The value of the gross estate shall include the value of all property to the extent of the interest therein of the decedent at the time of his death Sec. 20.2031-1 Definition of gross estate; valuation of property. (a) Definition of gross estate. Except as otherwise provided in this paragraph the value of the gross estate of a decedent who was a citizen or resident of the United States at the time of his death is the total value of the interests described in sections 2033 through 2044. The gross estate of a decedent who died before October 17, 1962, does not include real property situated outside the United States (as defined in paragraph (b)(1) of Sec. 20.0-1). Except as provided in paragraph (c) of this section (relating to the estates of decedents dying after October 16, 1962, and before July 1, 1964), in the case of a decedent dying after October 16, 1962, real property situated outside the United States which comes within the scope of sections 2033 through 2044 is included in the gross estate to the same extent as any other property coming within the scope of those sections. In arriving at the value of the gross estate the interests described in sections 2033 through 2044 are valued as described in this section, Secs. 20.2031-2 through 20.2031-9 and Sec. 20.2032-1. The contents of sections 2033 through 2044 are, in general, as follows: (1) Sections 2033 and 2034 are concerned mainly with interests in property passing through the decedent's probate estate. Section 2033 includes in the decedent's gross estate any interest that the decedent had in property at the time of his death. Section 2034 provides that any interest of the decedent's surviving spouse in the decedent's property, such as dower or curtesy, does not prevent the inclusion of such property in the decedent's gross estate. (2) Sections 2035 through 2038 deal with interests in property transferred by the decedent during his life under such circumstances as to bring the interests within the decedent's gross estate. Section 2035 includes in the decedent's gross estate property transferred in contemplation of death, even though the decedent had not interest in, or control over, the property at the time of his death. Section 2036 provides for the inclusion of transferred property with respect to which the decedent retained the income or the power to designate who shall enjoy the income. Section 2037 includes in the decedent's gross estate certain transfers under which the beneficial enjoyment of the property could be obtained only by surviving the decedent. Section 2038 provides for the inclusion of transferred property if the decedent had at the time of his death the power to change the beneficial enjoyment of the property. It should be noted that there is considerable overlap in the application of sections 2036 through 2038 with respect to reserved powers, so that transferred property may be includible in the decedent's gross estate in varying degrees under more than one of those sections. (3) Sections 2039 through 2042 deal with special kinds of property and powers. Sections 2039 and 2040 concern annuities and jointly held property respectively. Section 2041 deals with powers held by the decedent over the beneficial enjoyment of property not originating with the decedent. Section 2042 concerns insurance under policies on the life of the decedent. (4) Section 2043 concerns the sufficiency of consideration for transfers made by the decedent during his life. This has a bearing on the amount to be included in the decedent's gross estate under sections 2035 through 2038, and 2041. Section 2044 deals with retroactivity. Sec. 20.2033-1 Property in which the decedent had an interest. (a) In general. The gross estate of a decedent who was a citizen or resident of the United States at the time of his death includes under section 2033 the value of all property, whether real or personal, tangible or intangible, and wherever situated, beneficially owned by the decedent at the time of his death. (For certain exceptions in the case of real property situated outside the United States, see paragraphs (a) and (c) of Sec. 20.2031-1.) Real property is included whether it came into the possession and control of the executor or administrator or passed directly to heirs or devisees. Various statutory provisions which exempt bonds, notes, bills, and certificates of indebtedness of the Federal Government or its agencies and the interest thereon from taxation are generally not applicable to the estate tax, since such tax is an excise tax on the transfer of property at death and is not a tax on the property transferred. (b) Miscellaneous examples. A cemetery lot owned by the decedent is part of his gross estate, but its value is limited to the salable value of that part of the lot which is not designed for the interment of the decedent and the members of his family. Property subject to homestead or other exemptions under local law is included in the gross estate. Notes or other claims held by the decedent are likewise included even though they are cancelled by the decedent's will. Interest and rents accrued at the date of the decedent's death constitute a part of the gross estate. Similarly, dividends which are payable to the decedent or his estate by reason of the fact that on or before the date of the decedent's death he was a stockholder of record (but which have not been collected at death) constitute a part of the gross estate. Because the estate tax is an excise on the act of transferring wealth at death, the tax base must include the value of the property that most obviously is transferred: property directly owned by the decedent Current 2033 originated in the Revenue Act of 1926, and it brings into the gross estate the value of all property to the extent of the interest therein of the decedent at the time of his death Section 2033 applies to the decedents securities, bank deposits, and real estate Rights to income that accrue before death and are collectible by the estate, such as rents, interest, dividends, partnership profit shares, refunds, and vested rights to death benefits are includable in the gross estate Also includable are promissory notes, whether matured or not, insurance policies owned by the decedent on the life of another, saleable leasehold interests, interests in property as tenant in common, and one half of community property Of course, if the decedent holds only bare legal title to property, as in the case of a trustee under an enforceable oral or written trust agreement, she does not possess a taxable interest Federal and state bonds are includable despite income tax exemption Property law sees a power as an agency, and in broad terms, a power of appointment is an authority created or reserved by a person (the donor) having property subject to his disposition, enabling the powerholder (the donee) to designate, within limits prescribed by the donor, who shall receive the property and in what manner As an agent, the donee does not own the appointive property directly, but he does hold one of the more significant attributes of an owner: the power to dispose Section 2041, therefore includes within the donees gross estate property subject at her death to a general power of appointment: one that is exercisable in favor of the donee, her estate, her creditors, or the creditors of her estate Helvering v. Safe Deposit & Trust Co. General powers of appointment Issue: The case presents two questions, the first whether the decedent at the time of his death had by virtue of his general powers of appointment, even if never exercised, such an interest in the trust property as to require its inclusion in his gross estate under 2033 The decedent died and at that time was the beneficiary of three trusts One created by his fathers will, one by deed executed by his mother, and one created by his mothers will Each of the trusts gave D a general testamentary power of appointment over the trust property Holding: Because the power was not exercised, the general power of appointment is not to be included in the decedents gross estate Viewing 2033 in its background of legislative, judicial, and administrative history, we cannot reach the conclusion that the words interest of the decedent at the time of his death, were intended by Congress to include property subject to a general testamentary power of appointment unexercised by the decedent Illustrative Material The power of appointment provisions were completely revised after the decision in Safe Deposit, and property subject to a general power of appointment created after 10/21/1942 is includable in the gross estate under 2041 regardless of whether it is exercised The Safe Deposit decision continues to be important because it indicates that 2033 is not to be read as broadly in the estate tax as 61 in the income tax Because the estate tax is an excise on the act of transfer, an interest in property is interpreted for purposes of 2033 in terms of whether the decedent had at death something that could be transfered to a successor If decedents interest came into existence during her life and is not cut off by death, the value of the interest is includable in her gross estate On the other hand, 2033 does not reach an interest that is terminated by death For example, if A transfers property in trust to B for life, remainder to C, or if C predeceases B, remainder to D, nothing is included in Bs estate at Bs death because Bs interest ends at her death Remainder interests that are extinguished at death are excluded on the same ground, so nothing is included in Cs estate if C dies while B is still alive Rev Rule 55-438: If D purchases a life annuity without refund or survivorship features, 2033 includes nothing Second National Bank of Danville v. Dallman Contingent beneficiary D had the right to receive an annuity of 3% per year for life on the principal amount of an insurance policy on the life of her father, and D could not receive any principal during her life but could designate a contingent beneficiary to receive the principal upon her death If D failed to appoint one, the principal was to be paid to her executor, which is what happened The Court held that D had neither a power of appointment nor an interest in the principal under 2033 The Court reasoned that the property passed to the executor under the contract with Ds father and not through any act of D The Service has refused to follow the Dallman decision in regards to both prongs, 2033 and 2041 In Keeter v. United States, the 5th Circuit reversed the district courts holding that a remainder payable to an income beneficiarys estate was not includable under either 2033 or 2041 In reversing on the power of appointment issue, the 5th Circuit stated: For estate tax purposes, the critical question is whether the decedent directed her property after her death, not how that property got into the position from which she could direct it. In terms of the actual authority of direction over the insurance proceeds that was conferred and exercised, we see no substantive differences among an express grant of a general power of appointment and a grant employing previously existing law that allows the donee to distribute property freely by will In Adriane v. Higgins, the corpus of a trust created by D was, upon his wifes death, payable to D or if he shall then be dead, to his legal representatives The Second Circuit held that the remainder was the practical equivalent of a reservation to himself and the reversion which he had thus created was his property, fully taxable under 2033, which provides for taxation of the gross estate to the extent of the interest therein of the decedent at the time of his death Revenue Ruling 67-370: S created a trust with remainder to D or his estate upon Ss death, and S reserved the right to revoke the trust at any time D dies and S modified the trust, eliminating the remainder interest of Ds estate The Service held that it is not relevant to the application of 2033 that a particular interest in property which survives the decedents death may be either defeasible or indefeasible Certainly, 2033 ought not to be thwarted just because a property interest may be undone by the occurrence of an event that is beyond anyones control Section 2033 and State Property Law Tangible and intangible property interest recognized by state law are covered by 2033. In both cases, the application of 2033 turns on whether the interest is covered by state law property concepts Interests Arising at Death (CB pg. 109) Connecticut Bank & Trust Co v. US After death Issue: Whether or not the value of an action for wrongful death is property of the decedent at the time of his death Decedents were killed in a car crash after being struck by a tractor trailer owned by HMH Motor Service During the following year, the executor commenced a wrongful death action against HMH, and the parties settled for $320,000 Holding: Where, as here, there was no property interest in the decedent which passed by virtue of his death, but rather one which arose after his death, such an interest is not property owned at death and not part of the gross estate under 2033 Simple logic mandates the conclusion that an action for wrongful death cannot exist until the decedent has died, at which point, he is no longer a person capable of owning any property interests What the estate tax taxes is not the interest to which the legatees and devisees succeeded on death, but the interest which ceased by reason of death Wrongful Death Statutes After several losses in other cases, the Service conceded that the value of proceeds of settlement or recovery under a survival type of wrongful death statute would not be includable under either 2033 or 2041 The holding under 2041 followed from the same legal point that controlled the 2033 outcome: because the wrongful death action cannot exist until D has died, there is no property over which D has a power of appointment at the time of his death If, however, the proceeds represent damages to which D became entitled during his lifetime (e.g. for pain and suffering or medical expenses) the Service will include their value under 2033 Also, Revenue Ruling 83-44 held that amounts paid to Ds estate as a basic economic loss benefit under a no fault insurance policy, which D was required to maintain and which covered Ds medical expenses and loss of income arising from Ds injury while operating a motor vehicle were includable under 2033 The result is correct because D had a contractual right to recovery during life that survived him, and the amount actually recovered after death measured the value of that right Property vs. Expectancy (CB pg. 113) Estate of Barr v. Commissioner Expectancy Issue: Whether the so called wage dividend death benefit is includible in the gross estate of the decedent for Federal estate purposes, under either 2033 or 2039 Decedents employer had long paid employees on the payroll the last day of the fiscal year a wage dividend in the following March if profits permitted To be eligible for a wage dividend, an employee had to be alive and employed by the company on the last day of the Kodak year In addition, if before qualifying an employee died and was not survived by a spouse, child, or parent the company may, at its option, pay a wage dividend to the estate or other beneficiary that the officers may select If an employee dies after having qualified for a wage dividend, but before receiving it, a wage dividend is paid to the estate as a matter of right Holding: It is our opinion that, in the present case, the decedent did not have at the time of his death any property interest, either in the wage dividend which Eastmans board of directors subsequently declared for the benefit of its living eligible employees or in the related death benefit which these directors then authorized to be paid to the decedents widow. Accordingly, there was no such interest which passed, or could have passed, from him to his widow, and hence no such interest upon which the excise tax on the privilege of transmitting property at death may be imposed under 2033 It will be observed that 2033 relates only to interests in property which the decedent had at the time of his death Both this court and others have recognized that there is a distinction between rights of an employee to death benefits, and, on the other hand, mere hopes and expectancies on the part of an employee that death benefits may be paid We are convinced that in the instant case, decedent had no more than a hope or expectancy that his surviving spouse might receive a wage dividend death benefit There were so many events that had to occur before such hope could be realized that we find it impossible to conclude that, at the date of death, he had any property right which he could pass to her Vested Rights to Benefits Benefits payable on account of death are includable in the gross estate of a deceased employee under 2033, if at the time of death, the employee had a legally enforceable vested right to the benefits As illustrated by Estate of Barr, an employers policy of paying death benefits, as evidenced by a consistent history of payment, coupled with actual payment, do not create an includable interest, as long as the employer retains discretion over whether to make the payment If D contracts with her employer for the payments of death benefits directly to her husband or other irrevocably named beneficiaries on the sole condition that she remain employed by that employer at the time of her death, the value of the death benefits paid is not includible under 2033 because D had no rights to receive the benefits herself Employee death benefits may be includable under 2033 when they are paid from separate trusts funded by the employee Section 2033 applies where the source of the death benefits was a qualified employee retirement trust to which the employee made contributions that were immediately credited to his account subject to defeasance only upon circumstances within his control Effect of Ability to Designate Beneficiaries The Courts frequently refer to the decedents ability to designate beneficiaries as a factor relevant to inclusion of death benefits under 2033, and although this factor is relevant under 2037, 2039, and 2041, it is of questionable importance under 2033 Payments in the Nature of Deferred Compensation Some cases have required the inclusion of death benefits that were found to represent deferred compensation for the decedents services Other courts have included death benefits under 2033 as deferred compensation where the source of the benefits was Ds own contribution of salary that she otherwise would have been entitled to receive currently Bank Deposits, Checks, and Notes (CB pg. 119) Estate of Belcher v. Commissioner Checks Issue: Are the amount of the checks in question includible in the gross estate of the decedent under either 2031 or 2033? In mid December 1973, Belcher drew 36 checks on his bank account to give to charities, and the checks along with the accompanying letters were sent on about 12/21 and there was sufficient funds in the account These checks cleared the drawee bank in January 1974 Belcher died 12/31, and the executors did not take any action to stop payment on the checks, nor did they take any action to recover the proceeds of the checks from the donees On decedents Federal estate tax return, the executors reported only the net amount of cash on deposit in the account on 12/31/1973, reduced by the $94,960 in checks outstanding on that date Holding: We hold that the amount of the checks in question is not includable in the gross estate under 2031 or 2033 Upon prompt presentation and actual payment of the checks by the bank, the conditional payment which occurred when the checks were issued became absolute and related back in time when the checks were delivered, thereby eliminating the amount represented by the checks from the assets of the decedent at the time of her death Regulation 20.2031-5 provides: The fair market value of notes, secured or unsecured, is presumed to be the amount of unpaid principal, plus interest accrued to the date of death, unless the executor establishes that the value is lower or that the notes are worthless. However, items of interest shall be separately stated on the estate tax return. If not returned at face value, plus accrued interest, satisfactory evidence must be submitted that the note is worth less than the unpaid amount (because of the interest rate, date of maturity, or other cause), or that the note is uncollectible, either in whole or in part (by reason of the insolvency of the party or parties liable, or for other cause), and that any property pledged or mortgaged as security is insufficient to satisfy the obligation. In this case, none of the pledges were intended and therefore the checks in question were not given in discharge of bona fide legal obligations of the decedent The court believes that the above quoted regulation does not apply to this case because it does not contemplate this situation No such policy consideration would justify the inclusion of outstanding checks issued in good faith by the decedent to the charitable donees prior to her death but cashed after her death The inclusion of these checks would at the very least create the possibility that they would qualify as 2055 charitable deductions, again creating a wash Doesnt apply to non-charitable checks. Checks Judge Chabots dissent in Belcher appears correct in its technical aspect The relation back and mailing constitutes payment rules are justified on administrative grounds in the income tax context, where the timing both of a charitable deduction and of a cash method taxpayers other deductions depend on when payment is made But adoption of the rule that, upon mailing an item is deemed paid within the meaning of 170, 162, 163, etc., does not say that, if the check is not cashed before death, the money in fact is in the decedents account, and, even if mailing the check were a transfer, the decedent had the power up to the moment of death to revoke the transfer by stopping payment In its Action on Decision in Belcher, the Service indicated that it would not apply the relation back doctrine in estate tax cases involving non-charitable donees The Service will read Belcher as creating a limited, equitable exception to the regulations for checks issued in good faith to charitable donees, and will continue to apply Reg. 20.2031-5 in cases involving gift checks to non-charitable donees (like gifts to family members: Rosano v. US) Notes A note held by the decedent is includible in the gross estate under 2033 even though the note is cancelled by the decedents will In Estate of Moss v. Commissioner, however, the court held that where a note is extinguished at death by a previously entered into contract, the notes would not be includible in the decedents estate State Gift Taxes While state gift taxes treated as prepaid inheritance taxes would not be included in a decedents gross estate, they would also not qualify for the state death tax credit under 2011 Right to Accrued Payments (CB pg. 130) Income earned by uncollected at death is property for purposes of 2033, regardless of the nature of the income right If Ds income entitlements have matured prior to death, but have not been recognized in the technical income tax sense, 691(a) characterizes the amount as income in respect of a decedent and includes them, upon receipt, in the gross income of the estate or other beneficiary who ultimately receives them Chapter 13 The Scope of the Gift Tax: 2501 and 2511 Introductory Note (CB pg. 133) Sec. 2501. Imposition of tax (a) Taxable transfers (1) General rule - A tax, computed as provided in section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-12-A-2502.html\" 2502, is hereby imposed for each calendar year on the transfer of property by gift during such calendar year by any individual resident or nonresident. Sec. 2511. Transfers in general (a) Scope - Subject to the limitations contained in this chapter, the tax imposed by section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-12-A-2501.html\" 2501 shall apply whether the transfer is in trust or otherwise, whether the gift is direct or indirect, and whether the property is real or personal, tangible or intangible; but in the case of a nonresident not a citizen of the United States, shall apply to a transfer only if the property is situated within the United States. Sec. 2512. Valuation of gifts (a) If the gift is made in property, the value thereof at the date of the gift shall be considered the amount of the gift. (b) Where property is transferred for less than an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth, then the amount by which the value of the property exceeded the value of the consideration shall be deemed a gift, and shall be included in computing the amount of gifts made during the calendar year. (c) Cross reference - For individual's right to be furnished on request a statement regarding any valuation made by the Secretary of a gift by that individual, see section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-F-77-7517.html\" 7517. Sec. 25.2501-1 Imposition of tax. (a) In general. (1) The tax applies to all transfers by gift of property, wherever situated, by an individual who is a citizen or resident of the United States, to the extent the value of the transfers exceeds the amount of the exclusions authorized by section 2503 and the deductions authorized by sections 2521 (as in effect prior to its repeal by the Tax Reform Act of 1976), 2522, and 2523. For each ``calendar period'' (as defined in Sec. 25.2502-1(c)(1)), the tax described in this paragraph (a) is imposed on the transfer of property by gift during such calendar period. Sec. 25.2511-1 Transfers in general. (a) The gift tax applies to a transfer by way of gift whether the transfer is in trust or otherwise, whether the gift is direct or indirect, and whether the property is real or personal, tangible or intangible. For example, a taxable transfer may be effected by the creation of a trust, the forgiving of a debt, the assignment of a judgment, the assignment of the benefits of an insurance policy, or the transfer of cash, certificates of deposit, or Federal, State or municipal bonds. Statutory provisions which exempt bonds, notes, bills and certificates of indebtedness of the Federal Government or its agencies and the interest thereon from taxation are not applicable to the gift tax, since the gift tax is an excise tax on the transfer, and is not a tax on the subject of the gift. See Reg. 25.2511-1(c)-(h) Sec. 25.2512-8 Transfers for insufficient consideration. Transfers reached by the gift tax are not confined to those only which, being without a valuable consideration, accord with the common law concept of gifts, but embrace as well sales, exchanges, and other dispositions of property for a consideration to the extent that the value of the property transferred by the donor exceeds the value in money or money's worth of the consideration given therefor. However, a sale, exchange, or other transfer of property made in the ordinary course of business (a transaction which is bona fide, at arm's length, and free from any donative intent), will be considered as made for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth. A consideration not reducible to a value in money or money's worth, as love and affection, promise of marriage, etc., is to be wholly disregarded, and the entire value of the property transferred constitutes the amount of the gift. Similarly, a relinquishment or promised relinquishment of dower or curtesy, or of a statutory estate created in lieu of dower or curtesy, or of other marital rights in the spouse's property or estate, shall not be considered to any extent a consideration ``in money or money's worth.'' See, however, section 2516 and the regulations thereunder with respect to certain transfers incident to a divorce. See also sections 2701, 2702, 2703 and 2704 and the regulations at Secs. 25.2701-0 through 25.2704-3 for special rules for valuing transfers of business interests, transfers in trust, and transfers pursuant to options and purchase agreements. Section 2501(a) imposes the gift tax on the transfer of property by gift, and 2511(a) provides that the gift tax shall apply whether the gift is in trust or otherwise, whether the gift is direct or indirect, and whether the property is or personal, tangible or intangible The committee reports accompanying enactment of the gift tax in 1932 explain that the terms transfer, property, gift, and indirect are used in the broadest and most comprehensive sense Is There a Transfer of Property (CB pg. 134) Compensation Free Services (pg. 134) Commissioner v. Hogle Stockbroker commissions Issue: Whether or not annual earnings of trusts from trading in securities and commodities carried on by the trusts under Hogles direction, amounted to gifts by Hogle to the trusts Parent (a successful stockbroker) set up irrevocable trusts with independent trustees for the benefit of his three children In Hogle I, the 10th Circuit held that income from trading on margin was taxable to Parent rather than to the trusts because Parent controlled the amount of income through the exercise of his personal skill and judgment in trading the securities The Service then sought to impose a gift tax on the theory that the income from margin trading thus was earned by Parent and subsequently given to the trusts Holding: We do not think that the income in each of the taxable years derived from trading constituted a gift thereof by Hogle to the trusts The net income derived from trading carried on in behalf of the trusts accrued immediately and directly to the trusts, and did not consist of income accruing to Hogle which he transferred by anticipatory gift to the trusts Hogle never owned or held an economic interest in such income What, in fact and in reality, Hogle gave to the trusts in the taxable years was his expert services in carrying on the trading, personal services in the management of the trusts He could not withhold from the trusts any of the income accruing from trading on margin, thus how could he give what he could not withhold There was thus no transfer The Property v. Services Distinction Subsequent cases and rulings rely on Hogle II for the proposition that the performance of gratuitous services is not subject to the gift tax Hogle II has been interpreted by Crown, Estate of Childers and other cases to mean that the value of Parents investment services was not subject to the gift tax because there was no property for 2501(a) purposes The statutory rationale is that by referring only to property, 2501 and 2511 implicitly exempt gifts of services This is so even though the gift tax is used to backstop the estate tax to reach inter vivos transfers that deplete the donors estate Revenue Ruling 66-167: Received as fees Issue: Whether the amounts which the taxpayer-executor could have received as fees or commissions are includible in his gross income and whether his waiver of the right to receive these amounts results in a gift The taxpayer served as the sole executor of his deceased wifes estate pursuant to the terms of a will under which he and his adult son were each given a half interest in the net proceeds thereof At some point within a reasonable time after first entering upon the performance of his duties as executor, the taxpayer decided to make no charge for serving in such capacity Holding: The amounts which the present taxpayer would have otherwise become entitled to receive as fees or commissions are not includible in his gross income and are not gifts The crucial test of whether the executor of an estate or any other fiduciary in a similar situation may waive his right to receive statutory commissions without thereby incurring any income or gift tax liability is whether the waiver involved will at least primarily constitute evidence of an intent to render a gratuitous service If the timing, purpose, and effect of the waiver make it serve any other important objective, it may then be proper to conclude that the fiduciary has thereby enjoyed a realization of income by means of controlling the disposition thereof, and at the same time, has also effected a taxable gift by means of any resulting transfer to a third party of his contingent beneficial interest in a part of the assets under his fiduciary control The requisite intention to serve on a gratuitous basis will ordinarily be deemed to have been adequately manifested if the executor or administrator of an estate supplies one or more of the decedents legatees or devisees, or of those principally entitled to distribution of the decedents estate, within 6 months after his initial appointment as such fiduciary, with a formal waiver of any right to compensation for his services This could also be accomplished through an implied waiver Policy Issues Rev Ruling 64-225 state that a waiver, express or implied, of the compensation for services after it has been earned is insufficient to prevent the compensation from constituting income to the earner and hence the waiver constitutes a gift of that income Interest Free Loans and Rent Free Use of Other Assets (CB pg. 139) Introductory Note Sec. 7872. Treatment of loans with below-market interest rates (a) Treatment of gift loans and demand loans (1) In general - For purposes of this title, in the case of any below-market loan to which this section applies and which is a gift loan or a demand loan, the forgone interest shall be treated as (A) transferred from the lender to the borrower, and (B) retransferred by the borrower to the lender as interest. (2) Time when transfers made - Except as otherwise provided in regulations prescribed by the Secretary, any forgone interest attributable to periods during any calendar year shall be treated as transferred (and retransferred) under paragraph (1) on the last day of such calendar year. (b) Treatment of other below-market loans (1) In general - For purposes of this title, in the case of any below-market loan to which this section applies and to which subsection (a)(1) does not apply, the lender shall be treated as having transferred on the date the loan was made (or, if later, on the first day on which this section applies to such loan), and the borrower shall be treated as having received on such date, cash in an amount equal to the excess of (A) the amount loaned, over (B) the present value of all payments which are required to be made under the terms of the loan. (2) Obligation treated as having original issue discount - For purposes of this title (A) In general - Any below-market loan to which paragraph (1) applies shall be treated as having original issue discount in an amount equal to the excess described in paragraph (1). (B) Amount in addition to other original issue discount - Any original issue discount which a loan is treated as having by reason of subparagraph (A) shall be in addition to any other original issue discount on such loan (determined without regard to subparagraph (A)). (c) Below-market loans to which section applies (1) In general - Except as otherwise provided in this subsection and subsection (g), this section shall apply to (A) Gifts - Any below-market loan which is a gift loan. (B) Compensation-related loans - Any below-market loan directly or indirectly between (i) an employer and an employee, or (ii) an independent contractor and a person for whom such independent contractor provides services. (C) Corporation-shareholder loans - Any below-market loan directly or indirectly between a corporation and any shareholder of such corporation. (D) Tax avoidance loans - Any below-market loan 1 of the principal purposes of the interest arrangements of which is the avoidance of any Federal tax. (E) Other below-market loans - To the extent provided in regulations, any below-market loan which is not described in subparagraph (A), (B), (C), or (F) if the interest arrangements of such loan have a significant effect on any Federal tax liability of the lender or the borrower. (F) Loans to qualified continuing care facilities - Any loan to any qualified continuing care facility pursuant to a continuing care contract. (2) $10,000 de minimis exception for gift loans between individuals (A) In general - In the case of any gift loan directly between individuals, this section shall not apply to any day on which the aggregate outstanding amount of loans between such individuals does not exceed $10,000. (B) De minimis exception not to apply to loans attributable to acquisition of income-producing assets - Subparagraph (A) shall not apply to any gift loan directly attributable to the purchase or carrying of income-producing assets. (C) Cross reference - For limitation on amount treated as interest where loans do not exceed $100,000, see subsection (d)(1). (3) $10,000 de minimis exception for compensation-related and corporate-shareholder loans (A) In general - In the case of any loan described in subparagraph (B) or (C) of paragraph (1), this section shall not apply to any day on which the aggregate outstanding amount of loans between the borrower and lender does not exceed $10,000. (B) Exception not to apply where 1 of principal purposes is tax avoidance - Subparagraph (A) shall not apply to any loan the interest arrangements of which have as 1 of their principal purposes the avoidance of any Federal tax. (d) Special rules for gift loans (1) Limitation on interest accrual for purposes of income taxes where loans do not exceed $100,000 (A) In general - For purposes of subtitle A, in the case of a gift loan directly between individuals, the amount treated as retransferred by the borrower to the lender as of the close of any year shall not exceed the borrower's net investment income for such year. (B) Limitation not to apply where 1 of principal purposes is tax avoidance - Subparagraph (A) shall not apply to any loan the interest arrangements of which have as 1 of their principal purposes the avoidance of any Federal tax. (C) Special rule where more than 1 gift loan outstanding - For purposes of subparagraph (A), in any case in which a borrower has outstanding more than 1 gift loan, the net investment income of such borrower shall be allocated among such loans in proportion to the respective amounts which would be treated as retransferred by the borrower without regard to this paragraph. (D) Limitation not to apply where aggregate amount of loans exceed $100,000 - This paragraph shall not apply to any loan made by a lender to a borrower for any day on which the aggregate outstanding amount of loans between the borrower and lender exceeds $100,000. Dickman v. Commissioner Interest free demand loans Issue: Whether intrafamily, interest free demand loans result in taxable gifts of the value of the use of the money lent Paul and Esther Dickman made over $1.3 million in interest free demand loans over a five year period to their son and to a family owned business Holding: We hold that the interest free demand loans shown by this record resulted in taxable gifts of the reasonable value of the use of the money lent The statutory language of the federal gift tax provisions purports to reach any gratuitous transfer of any interest in property Section 2511(a) highlights the broad sweep of the tax imposed by 2501 providing in pertinent part: Subject to the limitations contained in this chapter, the tax imposed by 2501 shall apply whether the transfer is in trust or otherwise, whether the gift is direct or indirect, and whether the property is real, personal, or intangible The Court also noted that the language of the gift tax statute is broad enough to include property, however conceptual or contingent The taxable gift that assertedly results from an interest free demand loan is the value of receiving and using the money without incurring a corresponding obligation to pay interest along with the loans repayment We have little difficulty accepting the theory that the use of valuable property, in this case money, is itself a legally protectible property interest, because of the aggregate rights associated with any property interest, the right of use of property is perhaps of the highest order The value of the use of money is found in what it can produce; the measure of that value is interest rent for the use of the funds Against this backdrop, the gift tax statutes clearly encompass within their broad sweep the gratuitous transfer of the use of money The Courts holding that an interest free demand loan results in a taxable gift of the use of the transferred funds is fully consistent with one of the major purposes of the federal gift tax statute: protection of the estate tax and the income tax A substantial no interest loan from parent to child creates significant tax benefits for the lender quite apart from the economic advantages to the borrower This is especially so when an individual in a high income tax bracket transfers income producing property to an individual in a lower tax bracket, thereby reducing the taxable income of the highest taxpayer, at the expense, ultimately of all other taxpayers and the government A gratuitous transfer of income producing property may enable the transferor to avoid the future estate tax liability that would result if the earnings generated by the property became a part of the transferors estate Scope of Dickman As Justice Powell pointed out in the dissent, the majority did not specifically limit its holding to interest free loans of money and he thus noted that potential tax liability may arise in a wide range of situations involving the unrecompensed use of property Legislative Response: 7862 In 1984, Congress recognized that in many instances the failure of the tax laws to treat below market loans in accordance with their economic substance provided taxpayers with opportunities to circumvent well established tax rules Under 7872 interest forgone on a below market loan is treated as transferred from the lender to the borrower and then re-transferred by the borrower to the lender as interest Demand Loan Below market loan if interest is payable on the loan at a rate less than the applicable federal rate In the case of a demand below market loan, foregone interest is treated for gift tax purposes as having been transferred from the lender to the borrower on the last day of the calendar year Term Loan A below market loan if the amount loaned exceeds the present value of all payments due The amount of the gift is determined at the time of the transfer and is equal to the excess of the amount loaned over the present value of all the payments that are required to be made under terms of the loan Gift Loan Any below market loan where the foregoing of interest is the nature of the gift The effect of 7872 on gift loans is to treat the amount of the foregone interest as a taxable gift from the lender to the borrower, create interest expense for the borrower in an amount equal to the foregone interest, and include in the lenders income an identical amount When is the Transfer Completed? (CB pg 153) Executory Agreements to Transfer (PG. 153) Commissioner v. Estate of Copley Completion of gifts Issue: Were the gifts completed in the year when the taxpayer promised to make the transfers or in the years when he actually made the transfers? In contemplation of marriage and in consideration thereof the decedent and his intended wife entered into an Antenuptial agreement by which Copley agreed to pay his intended wife upon marriage the sum of one million dollars to be her separate property The agreement continued that in consideration of the payment to her of the sum of one million dollars, the intended wife agreed to accept and does accept the sum of one million dollars in lieu of any and all rights The Commissioner contends that the gift tax was applicable to the years 1936 and 1944 when the payments were made to the wife, while the decedent argues that is should be in 1931 when the promise was made Holding: The gift is for the year 1931, when the obligation became binding and legally enforceable In 1931 Copley obligated himself unconditionally to pay his wife immediately after the solemnization of said marriage The obligation to pay his wife one day after marriage was a debt and possessed all the indicia of a promissory note payable on demand She could have assigned and conveyed good title to the instrument which evidenced his obligation to pay It is contrary to reason and common sense to say that the delivery of such property by Copley in 1936 and 1944, and its acceptance by his wife, was other than a discharge of the 1931 obligation Certainly, when the asserted transfers were made, marriage had nothing to do with the consideration that has been consummated in 1931 and it is equally certain that there was no relinquishment of marital rights because that also was consummated at the time of marriage, all of which emphasizes that the asserted transfers in 1935 and 1944 were none other than the discharge of a contractual obligation The key to the analysis to determine when a gift is completed is when one gives up dominion and control Because the gift tax was not in effect in 1931, the transfer being completed in 1931 is not subject to the gift tax Revenue Ruling 98-21: Nonstatutory stock options Issue: When is the transfer of a nonstatutory stock option by the optionee to a family member, for no consideration, a completed gift under 2511? Company grants to A, in consideration fro services to be performed by A, a nonstatutory stock option to purchase shares of Company common stock The stock options were only exercisable by A after completion of the additional services Before A performed the additional services necessary to allow As options to be exercised, A transfers As options to B, one of As children for no consideration Holding: The transfer to a family member, for no consideration, of a nonstatutory stock option, is a completed gift under 2511 on the later of the transfer, or the time when the donees right to exercise the option is no longer conditioned on the performance of services by the transferor The gift tax imposed by 2501 is a tax of which the donor is primarily and personally liable and is an excise upon the donors act of making the transfer, and is measured by the value of the property passing from the donor, and attaches regardless of the fact that the identity of the donee mat not then by known or ascertainable Generally, a gift is complete when the donor has so parted with dominion and control over the property as to leave the donor no power to change its disposition, whether for the donors own benefit or for the benefit of another In the present case, Company grants to A a nonstatutory stock option conditioned on the performance of additional services, and if A fails to perform the services, the option cannot be exercised Therefore, before A performs the services, the rights that A possess in the stock option have not acquired the character of enforceable property rights susceptible of transfer for federal gift tax purposes Time of Completion in General Reg. 25.2511-2(a) states the general rule that a gift is complete as to any property of which the donor has so parted with dominion and control as to leave in him no power to change its disposition, whether for his own benefit or for another The Service thus accepts the view that the gist takes place, if at all, at the time a legally binding obligation is created, provided the gift is susceptible of valuation at that time Stock Options Rev Rul 98-21 treats a transfer of stock options as a completed gift when the option becomes legally binding and enforceable Gifts of Notes and Checks The effective date of completion occurs at the time the donor can no longer revoke the gift, or revest the beneficial title to the property himself or change the interest of the beneficiaries. A mere promise to make a gift is not a gift The gift of a check does not become complete until it is paid, certified, or accepted by the drawee, or is negotiated for value to a third person. Date on which check is cashed Gift of a note was effective when paid The delivery of a check to a noncharitable donee will be deemed to be a completed gift for federal gift and estate tax purposes on the earlier of (i) the date on which the donor has so parted with dominion and control under local law as to leave in the donor no power to change its disposition, or (ii) the date on which the donee deposits the check (or cashes the check against available funds of the donee) or presents the check for payment, if it is established that: (1) the check was paid by the drawee bank when first presented to the drawee bank for payment; (2) the donor was alive when the check was paid by the drawee bank; (3) the donor intended to make a gift; (4) delivery of the check by the donor was unconditional; and (5) the check was deposited, cashed, or presented in the calendar year for which completed gift treatment is sought and within a reasonable time of issuance. Gifts of Stock Certificates Rev Ruling 54-135 held that where a donor endorses a certificate of stock and delivers it to his broker with directions to have it transferred to a donee, the transfer is a completed gift on the date the stock is transferred to the donee in the books of the corporation However, where the donor endorses the stock certificate in favor of a donee and physically delivers the certificate to the donee, the gift is completed on the date of delivery Joint and Mutual Wills Hambleton Because the joint will did not purport to restrict her disposition of her property in the separate trust, she had made only a contractual promise to transfer an indefinite amount of property at her death which is not a present gift of the remainder interest Pyle Since the taxpayers power to invade was, as construed by the court, limited by an ascertainable standard, the gift of the remainder interest was complete at the date of the taxpayers husbands death Transferred Interest Incapable of Valuation (CB pg. 168) Estate of DiMarco v. Commissioner Voluntary act Issue: Whether the present value of a survivors income benefit payable with respect to the decedent by decedents employer is an adjusted taxable gift within the meaning of 2001 IBM maintained a benefit plan consisting of group term life insurance and uninsured and unfunded survivors income benefit Decedent never had any power to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate the Plan in whole or in part IBM reserved the right, in its discretion, to modify the Plan if it determined that it was advisable to do so Holding: In our opinion, decedent never made a taxable gift of any interest in the survivors income benefit to his wife. It follows that the present value of the survivors income benefit is not an adjustable taxable gift within the meaning of 2001 The term adjustable taxable gifts is defined in 2001(b) as the total amount of the taxable gift made by the decedent after December 31, 1976, other than gifts which are includible in the gross estate of the decedent Section 2503(a) in turn defines taxable gifts as the total amount gifts made during the applicable period less certain statutory deductions Regulation 25.2511-2(f) provides that the relinquishment or termination of a power to change the beneficiaries of transferred property, occurring otherwise than by the death of the donor, is regarded as the event that completes the gift and causes the tax to apply This precludes a finding that the gift became complete for gift tax purposes by reason of decedents death We decline to hold that a transfer of property that becomes complete because the donors death makes it possible for the first time to value the transferred property is subject to the gift tax The Court also question whether incapability to value a gift even matters at all if dominion and control are at issue The clear language of the statute and the regulations requires that transferred property be valued for gift tax purposes at the time the transfer becomes complete Section 2512(a) provides that, in the case of a gift, the value thereof at the date of the gift shall be considered the amount of the gift The Court also agreed with the taxpayer that decedent never made a taxable gift of any property interest in the survivors income benefit because there was no act by decedent that qualifies as an act of transfer of an interest in property His participation in the plan was involuntary, he had no power to select or change the beneficiaries of the survivors income benefit, no power to alter the amount or timing of the payment We do not believe that a taxable event can occur for gift tax purposes unless there is first and in fact an act of transfer by the donor; and there can be no act of transfer unless the act is voluntary and the transferor has some awareness that he is in fact making a transfer of property Indirect Transfers (CB pg. 175) Heyen v United States - From one to another Issue: Was the transfers of stock by the decedent subject to the gift tax? Decedent transferred 115 shares of stock to six persons After receiving the stock certificates, all six signed the stock certificates in blank, and gave them to the plaintiff or the bank Subsequently, the bank cancelled the certificates and the stock was reissued to members of the decedents family Holding: The evidence amply shows that the stock transfers were indirect gifts to decedents family which were subject to the gift tax A gift tax will be imposed on any transfers of property by gift, and the tax applies to any transfer, whether direct or indirect, if the gift value is greater than the statutory exclusions The language of the gift tax statutes clearly provides that transfers of property by gift, by whatever means affected, are subject to the federal gift tax Regulation 25.2511-1(g)(1) does not preclude consideration of decedents actual intent to transfer the stock to family members, and 2511(a) requires consideration of whether decedent made an indirect transfer The evidence at trial indicated that the decedent wanted to transfer the shares to his family members and did this through the initial recipients to creates exclusions to avoid paying gift tax on indirect gifts to the actual family members The fact that 2 initial recipients retained the shares, the decedents intent with all the shares was the same What is a Gift? (CB pg. 177) Donative Intent and Adequate and Full Consideration (CB pg. 177) Sec. 2512. Valuation of gifts (see above) Sec. 25.2512-8 Transfers for insufficient consideration. (see above) Commissioner v. Wemyss Donative intent Issue: Whether the stock given to Mrs. More was a taxable gift? Mrs. Mores deceased husband had set up two trusts, one-half the income of which was for the benefit of Mrs. More and the other half for that of the child with provision that, in the event of Mrs. Mores remarriage, her part of the income ceased and went to child On her unwillingness to suffer loss of her trust income through remarriage the parties entered upon an agreement whereby taxpayer would transfer to Mrs. More a block of shares of stock and within one month they were married The Commissioner determined that the amount of the stock was subject to the gift tax Holding: While recognizing that marriage was of course a valuable consideration to support a contract, marriage is not sufficient to satisfy the requirement of 2512(b) in that it was not consideration reducible to money value. Sections 2501(a) and 2512(b) are not disparate provisions, as Congress directed them to the same purpose, and they should not be separated in application Congress intended to use the term gift in its broadest and most comprehensive sense For purposes of the gift tax it not only dispensed with the test of donative intent it also formulated a much more workable test, that where property is transferred for less than an adequate and full consideration in money or moneys worth, the excess in such money value shall for purposes of the tax imposed by this title be deemed a gift On finding that a transfer in the circumstances of a particular case is not made in the ordinary course of business, the transfer becomes subject to the gift tax to the extent that it is not made for an adequate and full consideration in money or moneys worth In this case, the transfer to Mrs. More was not made at arms length and was not in the ordinary course of business Background Notes The Wemyss result is consistent with 2043, which provides that the release of marital rights does not constitute adequate consideration for estate tax purposes, and 2034 which requires inclusion in the decedents estate of all dower or curtesy interests of a surviving spouse Section 102(a) provides that gross income does not include the value of property acquired by gift and correspondingly, 1015(a) provides that the basis for computing gain on the sale of property acquired by gift is the adjusted basis of the donor The courts have consistently held that the concept of a gift in the gift tax is not to be imported into income tax Congress added 2503(e) to provide an unlimited exclusion for amounts paid by any person on behalf of an individual as tuition to an educational organization or to any person who provides medical care to an individual Transfers to a trust to pay for these does not count Political Contributions (CB pg. 185) Section 2501(a)(5) exempts from the gift tax transfers to political organizations as defined in 527(e)(1); however, transactions to political organizations are not exempted from the estate tax Chapter 14 Transfers of Property/Marital Rights Transfers Incident to Separation and Divorce (CB pg. 195) Sec. 2053. Expenses, indebtedness, and taxes c) Limitations (1) Limitations applicable to subsections (a) and (b) (A) Consideration for claims - The deduction allowed by this section in the case of claims against the estate, unpaid mortgages, or any indebtedness shall, when founded on a promise or agreement, be limited to the extent that they were contracted bona fide and for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth; except that in any case in which any such claim is founded on a promise or agreement of the decedent to make a contribution or gift to or for the use of any donee described in section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-IV-2055.html\" 2055 for the purposes specified therein, the deduction for such claims shall not be so limited, but shall be limited to the extent that it would be allowable as a deduction under section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-IV-2055.html\" 2055 if such promise or agreement constituted a bequest. Sec. 2043. Transfers for insufficient consideration (a) In general - If any one of the transfers, trusts, interests, rights, or powers enumerated and described in sections HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-III-2035.html\" 2035 to 2038, inclusive, and section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-III-2041.html\" 2041 is made, created, exercised, or relinquished for a consideration in money or money's worth, but is not a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth, there shall be included in the gross estate only the excess of the fair market value at the time of death of the property otherwise to be included on account of such transaction, over the value of the consideration received therefor by the decedent. (b) Marital rights not treated as consideration (1) In general - For purposes of this chapter, a relinquishment or promised relinquishment of dower or curtesy, or of a statutory estate created in lieu of dower or curtesy, or of other marital rights in the decedent's property or estate, shall not be considered to any extent a consideration \"in money or money's worth\". (2) Exception For purposes of section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-IV-2053.html\" 2053 (relating to expenses, indebtedness, and taxes), a- transfer of property which satisfies the requirements of paragraph (1) of section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-12-B-2516.html\" 2516 (relating to certain property settlements) shall be considered to be made for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth. Sec. 2516. Certain property settlements Where a husband and wife enter into a written agreement relative to their marital and property rights and divorce occurs within the 3-year period beginning on the date 1 year before such agreement is entered into (whether or not such agreement is approved by the divorce decree), any transfers of property or interests in property made pursuant to such agreement (1) to either spouse in settlement of his or her marital or property rights, or (2) to provide a reasonable allowance for the support of issue of the marriage during minority, shall be deemed to be transfers made for a full and adequate consideration in money or money's worth. Interests in property are commonly transferred when spouses obtain a divorce or legal separation, and under the gift tax, in these situations, the issue is whether the transfer of property is a gift or whether instead the transfer is for adequate consideration Under 2036-2038, these sections provide for inclusion in the gross estate of the value of certain inter vivos transfers of property in which the decedent has retained an interest or which do not take effect until the death of the decedent In each case however, transfers for adequate and full consideration are excepted, subject to the limitations of 2043 Section 2053, permits a deduction from the gross estate for claims against the estate to the extent that the claims are founded on an agreement and are for adequate and full consideration Harris v. Commissioner Divorce Issue: Whether Wemyss and Merrill require the imposition of the gift tax in the type of post nuptial settlement of property rights here involved Petitioner divorced here husband and they reached an understanding as respects to the unscrambling of their property interests, the settlement of all litigated claims to separate properties, the assumption of obligations, and the transfer of properties It was found that the property transferred to her husband exceeded the amount that she received, and the Commissioner assessed a gift tax on the theory that any rights which the husband might have given up by entering into the agreement could not be adequate and full consideration Holding: The transfers of property are not subject to the gift tax If the parties had without more gone ahead and voluntarily unraveled their business interests on the basis of this compromise, there would be no question that the gift tax is payable For there would have been a promise or agreement that effected a relinquishment of marital rights in property It therefore would fall under the ban of the provision of the estate tax which by judicial construction has been incorporated into the gift tax But the parties did not simply undertake a voluntary contractual division of their property interest, as they were faced with the fact that Nevada law not only authorized but instructed the divorce court to decree a just and equitable disposition of both the community and separate property of the parties Unless an absolute decree was entered by a Nevada court, there would be no settlement agreement Thus, the arrangement would not be a promise or agreement in the statutory sense, it would be wholly conditional upon the entry of the decree In each case it is the decree that creates the rights and the duties; and a decree is not a promise or agreement in any sense popular or statutory If the transfer of marital rights in property is effected by the parties, it is pursuant to a promise or agreement in the meaning of the statute If the transfer is effected by court decree, no promise or agreement of the parties is the operative fact In no realistic sense is a court decree a promise or agreement between the parties to a litigation Section 2516(1) provides statutory consideration for purposes of the gift tax for transfers of property to either spouse incident to a written agreement where a divorce occurs within the 3 year period beginning on the date one year before the agreement is entered into Section 2516 embraces transfers in settlement of marital or property rights, therefore if the conditions of 2516 are met, interspousal transfers in exchange for the release of marital rights will not be subject to gift tax even if the property transferred exceeds the value of support rights surrendered See Question 6 BIG PICTURE Four separate grounds exist to supply adequate consideration for inter vivos transfers that would otherwise be subject to gift tax: First: When applicable, 2516 creates statutory consideration for the transfer (3 year written agreement); Second: The release of support rights is consideration to the extent of their value; Third: The release of marital rights constitutes adequate consideration where the divorce court has the power to declare the settlement of all property rights or to vary the terms of the prior settlement agreement and does approve the agreement; AND Fourth: A presently enforceable claim to an outright portion of the spouses property upon divorce (since it is not encompassed in the other marital rights of 2043b1). Chapter 15 Transfers with Retained Powers and Rights Background and Statutory Framework of 2036-2038 (CB pg. 214) Sec. 2036. Transfers with retained life estate (a) General rule - The value of the gross estate shall include the value of all property to the extent of any interest therein of which the decedent has at any time made a transfer (except in case of a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth), by trust or otherwise, under which he has retained for his life or for any period not ascertainable without reference to his death or for any period which does not in fact end before his death (1) the possession or enjoyment of, or the right to the income from, the property, or (2) the right, either alone or in conjunction with any person, to designate the persons who shall possess or enjoy the property or the income therefrom. (b) Voting rights (1) In general - For purposes of subsection (a)(1), the retention of the right to vote (directly or indirectly) shares of stock of a controlled corporation shall be considered to be a retention of the enjoyment of transferred property. (2) Controlled corporation - For purposes of paragraph (1), a corporation shall be treated as a controlled corporation if, at any time after the transfer of the property and during the 3-year period ending on the date of the decedent's death, the decedent owned (with the application of section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-A-1-C-I-C-318.html\" 318), or had the right (either alone or in conjunction with any person) to vote, stock possessing at least 20 percent of the total combined voting power of all classes of stock. (3) Coordination with section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-III-2035.html\" 2035 - For purposes of applying section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-III-2035.html\" 2035 with respect to paragraph (1), the relinquishment or cessation of voting rights shall be treated as a transfer of property made by the decedent. (c) Limitation on application of general rule - This section shall not apply to a transfer made before March 4, 1931; nor to a transfer made after March 3, 1931, and before June 7, 1932, unless the property transferred would have been includible in the decedent's gross estate by reason of the amendatory language of the joint resolution of March 3, 1931 (46 Stat. 1516). Sections 2036-2038 seek to include in the gross estate inter vivos transfers that are essentially testamentary in nature They include in the gross estate lifetime transfers in which the transferor has retained certain powers or rights with respect to the transferred property or has structured the transfer so that it is not complete until the transferors death Congress adopted these sections to prevent individuals from avoiding the generally higher estate tax liability by making testamentary transfers in the form of lifetime gifts The policy objectives of these sections may be described as follows: An estate tax should be imposed upon property transferred during lifetime if the transferor still retained the economic benefit of the property An estate tax should be imposed upon property transferred during life if the transfer is essentially testamentary An estate tax should be imposed upon property transferred during life if the transferor reserves significant powers over the possession or enjoyment of the property Transfers with Retained Life Estates - 2036 (CB pg. 216) Introductory Note Section 2036(a) requires the inclusion of property in the gross estate where D has transferred property but retained: The right to the income from the property transferred; The possession or enjoyment of the property (including, as specified in 2036(b), retention of voting rights, in transferred shares of a controlled corporation); or The right, either alone or conjunction with any other person, to designate the person who shall possess or enjoy the property or the income therefrom D must have made a transfer, she must have retained one of the above described interests, and the interest must be retained for life, for a period that is not ascertainable without reference to her death, or for a period that does not in fact end before her death There are two exceptions to 2036: A transfer for adequate and full consideration in money or moneys worth; and A transfer before 3/4/1931 Transfers Covered by 2036: In General (CB pg. 217) Estate of Nicol v. Commissioner The scope of 2036 Issue: Did the Commissioner properly include in the value of the decedents gross estate the farm land at issue? Decedent owned a farm which she leased to her daughter and son in law Decedent agreed to pay all property taxes and 1/3 of the cost of raising grain crops, and in return, decedent was entitled to receive 1/3 of the crops grown on the property The lease further provided that if the decedent conveyed any part of the leased property to her daughter, the lease would remain in effect A few days later, decedent did transfer one parcel of her farm Holding: We think these facts are sufficient to require the inclusion of the value of the farm land in her taxable estate under 2036(a)(1) as a transfer of property under which decedent retained the possession or enjoyment of, or the right to income from, the property for a period which did not in fact end before death Even though decedent, shortly after signing the lease, transferred the farm to her daughter, decedent continued to receive the rent provided in the lease until she died This, as in the case of testamentary dispositions of property, the benefits of ownership did not pass from decedent to daughter until the time of decedents death Section 2036 refers to any donative transfer under which the decedent has retained the enjoyment of property during his lifetime, and reflects a legislative policy of subjecting to tax all property which has been the subject of an incomplete donative inter vivos transfer Enjoyment as used in 2036(a)(1) is not a term of art, but is synonymous with substantial economic benefit Decedents retention of the right to the rent under the terms of the lease for a period which did not in fact end before her death brings the farm within the broad sweep of 2036 The period which does not in fact end before his death language contemplates a retention of income for such a period as to evidence decedents intention that it should extend at least for the duration of his life Reg. 20.2036-1(a) provides that if the decedent reserved an interest or right with respect to all of the property transferred by him, the amount to be included in his gross estate under 2036 is the value of the entire property If the property was split into A and B, and the property was transferred but a one-third interest in only B was retained, then the entire value of B is included, but none of A. The reservation does not have to be in writing or enforceable for 2036 to apply Transfers Under Section 2036 Horner v. United States Where an income beneficiary was entitled to $500 per month plus any amount of trust income above that amount that she requested in writing, her failure to request the additional income constituted a transfer because any income that she did not request became a part of the principal of the trust Gradow v. United States In community property states, when Ds will requires that the surviving spouse elect either to take her share of community property of let that share pass into Ds testamentary trust in which she is granted a life estate, her election to take the life estate constitutes a transfer Income from the property includes payment in kind In order for 2036 to be applicable,, the transferor must have held a sufficient interest in the property she transferred The IRS has ruled that a mere life estate does not constitute a sufficient interest in property for purposes of 2036 This makes sense because 2036 seeks to tax testamentary transfers, and the holder of a life estate cannot make a testamentary transfer since it will terminate at the transferors death In general, a transferors estate must include the proportion of property valued at the date of death or alternative valuation date with respect to which she reserved an interest or right The regulations permit a reduction for the value of any outstanding income interest which is not subject to decedents interest or right and which is actually being enjoyed by another person at the time of the decedents death This means that where D retained a secondary life estate in transferred property, his estate included the value of the property reduced by the value of the first tenants interest (See Ex. pg. 222) Where a grantor retains a secondary life estate, he need not actually come into enjoyment of the income by surviving the first life tenant in order to make the transfer subject to 2036 For example, suppose D transfers property to spouse S for life, then to D for life, remainder to children C. If D dies before S, the value of the property less the value of Ss life estate is still includible in Ds estate because the period for which D has retained an interest cannot be ascertained without reference to his death United States v. Estate of Grace Reciprocal trusts Issue: Was the value of the trust created by the decedents wife includible in his gross estate? On 12/15/1931, decedent executed a trust instrument, and the trustees were directed to pay the income of the trust to his wife during her lifetime, and to pay her any part of the principal which a majority of the trustees might deem advisable On 12/30/1931, decedents wife executed a trust instrument, which was virtually identical to the trust created by decedent, except the decedent was designated as the life beneficiary When the decedent died neither trust was included in his gross estate, and the Commissioner determined that the trusts created were reciprocal and the amount of the trust created by decedents wife should be included in decedents gross estate Holding: The value of the trust created by decedents wife is to be included in the value of the decedents gross estate Section 2036 provides that certain transferred property in which a decedent retained a life interest was to be included in his gross estate The doctrine of reciprocal trusts was formulated in response to attempts to draft instruments which seemingly avoid the literal terms of 2036 while still leaving the decedent the life time enjoyment of his property The taxability of a trust corpus does not hinge on a settlers motives, but depends on the nature and operative effect of the trust transfer We hold that application of the reciprocal trust doctrine requires only that the trusts be interrelated, and that the arrangement, to the extent of mutual value, leaves the settlors in approximately the same position as they would have been in had they created trusts naming themselves as life beneficiaries It is undisputed that in the instant case the two trusts are interrelated They are substantially identical in terms and were created at approximately the same time It is also clear that the transfers in trust left each party, to the extent of mutual value, in the same objective economic position as before When the reciprocal trust doctrine is applicable, the amount to be included in the estate of each transferor must be determined See page 230 Retention of Right to Income from the Property: 2036(a)(1) Cain v. Commissioner Income Issue: Should the installment payments be included in the decedents estate? The majority shareholder in a corporation sold her stock to the corporation for $150,000, payable $600 down and $1000 a month for 144 months The payments however were to terminate at the death of the shareholder The shareholder dies prior to receiving all the payments and the Commissioner asserted that under 2036(a)(1) the decedents gross estate included the remaining unpaid installments Holding: Since we find no significant distinction between the case at hand and the cases cited by the Supreme Court, we conclude that the decedent retained neither possession, enjoyment nor the right to income from the transferred stock. Accordingly, we hold that the unpaid balance of the purchase price is not includable in the decedents gross estate The record is devoid of evidence tending to show that the decedent at any time obtained possession or control over the entire purchase price of $150,000; that Kings Inc., tended the entire purchase price to the decedent; that the purchase price was, in fact, segregated by King Inc from its other assets; that the books and records or King reflected the receipt of the purchase price from the decedent; or that the corporation actually possessed cash or liquid assets totaling enough to pay decedent It is clear that the decedent divested herself of title to and control over the stock, and King entered into immediate and complete possession of the property The obligation to pay was fixed and dependent solely upon the singular obligation of King **Where a decedent, not in contemplation of death, has transferred property to another in return for a promise to make periodic payments to the transferor for his lifetime, it has been held that these payments are not income from transferred property so as to include the property in the estate of the decedent In Cain, the court concluded that D had not retained an interest because the payments were not dependent on income from the transferred property Transfers in Discharge of a Legal Obligation Reg. 20.2036-1(b)(2) provides that D will be considered to have retained or reserved the use, possession, right to income or other enjoyment of the transferred property to the extent that the income is to be applied toward the discharge of a legal obligation of the decedent, or otherwise for his pecuniary benefit A legal obligation to support a dependent during the decedents lifetime constitutes a legal obligation If D is the grantor-trustee of a trust, his retention of the power to use the trust corpus or income to discharge his legal obligation will trigger inclusion under 2036(a)(1) When the grantor is not the trustee, his estate will still include the trust property if the trustees are required to use it or its income to satisfy the grantors obligations Regs 20.2036-1(b)(2) does not apply where the discretionary power to apply trust income for the support of Ds dependent is vested in an independent trustee Prudowsky v. Commissioner The court held that if D has a support obligation and transfers property to himself as custodian under the Uniform Gifts to Minors Act which permits funds to be used to satisfy his support obligations, D has retained a power that requires inclusion of the property in his gross estate Same analysis under the Uniform Transfers to Minors Act Retention of Possession or Enjoyment of the Property: 2036(a)(1) & 2036(b)(1) Estate of Linderme v. Commissioner Retention of house Issue: Whether the decedent retained the possession of enjoyment of his residence so as to being its value within his gross estate Decedent executed a quitclaim deed to the residence to his three sons and delivered the deed Decedent continued in exclusive possession of the residence until he entered the nursing home, and the residence was unoccupied from that time until he dies There was neither consideration of any sale or rental or, nor any effort to sell or rent, the residence during that interval, thus indicating that the property was being held available for decedents possible return From the date of the deed until his death, decedents funds were used to pay all the expenses relating to the property Holding: On the basis of the entire record herein, we are satisfied as our ultimate finding of fact reflects that, beyond the mere existence of the family relationship and there mere occupancy of the premises, decedent did have an understanding whereby he retained the exclusive use of the residence until his death. The property in question is thus includible in the decedents gross estate In the instant case, the decedent continued to occupy the residence to the exclusion of the donees or anyone else whose status stemmed from their rights in the property Surely that occupancy was as much an economic benefit as if the decedent had retained the property and obtained the income therefrom Additionally, such exclusive occupancy, while not necessarily determinative, should be accorded greater significance than co-occupancy in the process of evaluating the various facets of a particular situation in order to determine whether an understanding existed whereby a decedent would retain possession or enjoyment After such a transfer has been made, the settlor must be left with no present legal title in the property, no reversionary interest in that title, and no right to possess or to enjoy the property then or thereafter. In other words such a transfer must be immediate and out and out, and must be unaffected by whether the grantor lives or dies Rev Rul 70-155 accepted the distinction between exclusive occupancy cases and those in which D continues to occupy the residence with the donee when the donor and donee are husband and wife The Ruling stated that continued co-tenancy does not alone support an inference or understanding between husband and wife as to retained possession or enjoyment of the property by the donor spouse Where the donor and donee are not husband and wife, however, the Service asserts that continued co-tenancy does support an inference of an understanding between the parties D can avoid 2036 by paying the fair rental value for the continued occupancy of the transferred property; it establishes that D did not retain an interest Sometimes taxpayers will attempt to disguise the retained interest by paying rent and then getting a refund in some way Where D transferred income producing real property to his children pursuant to an oral agreement that he would retain for his lifetime the income therefrom, 2036 requires inclusion One of the most valuable incidents of income producing real estate is the rent which it yields, and he who receives the rent in fact enjoys the property United States v. Byrum, Executrix Retained voting rights The Tax Reform Act of 1976 amended 2036 to overrule this holding Retention of Voting Rights in Transferred Stock The Revenue Act of 1978 changed 2036 to its current form in 2036(b), limiting its scope to the transfer of stock in a controlled corporation where the transferor retains voting rights A controlled corporation is one in which D owned, actually or constructively at least 20% of the total combined voting power of all classes of stock at any time after the transfer of property and during the three year period ending on the date of Ds death If donor A owns 20 out of 100 outstanding voting shares of common stock of a corporation, the gift of 1 share in which voting rights are retained will invoke 2036(b) On the other hand, donor B, who owns 19 shares of the same corporation can give away all 19 shares, retaining the voting rights therein, and none of the shares will be included in the gross estate Section 2036(b) requires inclusion of stock in Ds gross estate even if the grantor can exercise the power to vote the stock only in a fiduciary capacity, such as trustee The rule of 2036(b) would not apply to the transfer of stock in a controlled corporation where the decedent could not vote the transferred stock Similarly, where the decedent owned both voting and nonvoting stock and transferred the nonvoting stock to another person, the rule does not apply to the nonvoting stock simply because of the decedents ownership of the voting stock Retention of Power to Designate Who Shall Possess or Enjoy the Property: 2036(a)(2) (CB pg. 249) United States v. OMalley Who shall enjoy Issue: Whether OMalley retained a power to designate the person who shall possess or enjoy the property or the income therefrom, and whether the property sought to be included, namely the portions of trust principal representing accumulated income, was the subject of a previous transfer OMalley created 5 irrevocable trusts for his children and wife He was one of three trustee, each trust of which provided that the trustees, in their sole discretion, could pay trust income to the beneficiary or accumulate the income, in which event it became part of the principal of the trust Holding: Under 2036(a)(2), the power over income retained by OMalley is sufficient to require the inclusion of the original corpus of the trust in his gross estate, and the accumulated income added to the principal is subject to the same power and is thus includable as well Section 2036(a)(2) requires the property to be included not only when the grantor himself has the right to its income, but also when he has the right to designate those who may possess and enjoy it Here, OMalley was empowered, with the other trustees, to distribute the trust income to the income beneficiaries or to accumulate it and add it to the principal, thereby denying to the beneficiaries the privilege of immediate enjoyment and conditioning their eventual enjoyment upon surviving the termination of the trust This is a significant power and of sufficient substance to be deemed the power to designate within the meaning of 2036(a)(2) In the case before us now, the grantor reserved the power to accumulate income or distribute the income This power he exercised by accumulating and adding income to principal and this same power he held until the moment of his death with respect to both the original principal and the accumulated income In these circumstances, 2036(a)(2) requires inclusion in OMalleys gross estate of all of the trust principal, including those portions representing accumulated income United States v. Byrum, Executrix Section 2036(a)(2) issues Issue: Are the actions of Byrum sufficient to include the trusts he created within his gross estate under 2036(a)(2)? Byrum created an irrevocable trust to which he transferred shares of stock in three closely held corporations The trust agreement vested in the trustee broad and detailed powers with respect to the control and management of the trust property These powers were exercisable in the trustees sole discretion, subject to certain rights reserved to Byrum: 1)to vote the shares of unlisted stock held, 2) to disapprove the sale or transfer of any trusts assets, 3) to approve investments and reinvestments, and 4)to remove the trustee and designate another trustee to serve as successor Holding: We conclude that Byrum did not have an uncontested de facto power to regulate the flow of dividends to the trust, much less the right to designate who was to enjoy the income from trust property, thus no inclusion under 2036(a)(2) This Court has never held that trust property must be included in a settlors gross estate solely because the settlor retained the power to manage trust assets It has been recognized that a settlors retention of broad powers of management does not necessarily subject an inter vivos trust to the federal estate tax It must be conceded that Byrum reserved no right in the trust instrument or otherwise Right connotes an ascertainable and legally enforceable power, and here the right ascribed to Byrum was the power to use his majority position and influence over corporate directors to regulate the flow of income to the trust That right was neither ascertainable nor legally enforceable and hence was not a right in any normal sense of the term The power to elect the directors conferred no legal right to command them to pay or not pay dividends However great his influence may have been with the corporate directors, their responsibilities were to all stockholders and were enforceable according to legal standards entirely unrelated to the needs of the trust or to his desires with respect thereto Thus, his alleged de facto power to control the flow of dividends to the trust was subject to business and economic variables over which he had little or no control Although vested with broad discretion in determining when, whether, and what amounts of dividends shall be paid, that discretion of a director is subject to legal constraints If, in obedience to the will of the majority stockholder, corporate directors disregard the interests of shareholders by accumulating earnings to an unreasonable extent, they are vulnerable to a derivative suit They are similarly vulnerable if they make an unlawful payment of dividends in the absence of net earnings or available surplus, or if they fail to exercise the requisite degree of care in discharging their duty to act only in the best interest if the corporation and its stockholders Byrum was similarly inhibited by a fiduciary duty from abusing his position as majority shareholder for personal or family advantage to the detriment of the corporation or the other stockholders Jennings v. Smith Appointing trustees Issue: Whether the value of the trust property should have been included in the gross estate Decedent established two trusts, which were irrevocable and reserved no beneficial interest to the settlor He and his two sons were named trustees; in case a vacancy were to occur, provisions were made for the appointment of a successor trustee having like powers, there were to always to be 3 trustees and they were authorized to act by majority vote At the end of each year during the life of the son, the trustees were to accumulate the net income by adding it to the capital of the trust but they were given the power, in their absolute discretion at any time during the year and prior to the amalgamation of that years net income into capital, to use all or any part of it from the benefit of the son or his issue provided the trustee shall determine that such distribution is reasonably necessary to enable the beneficiary in question to maintain himself and his family , if any, in comfort and in accordance with the station in life to which he belongs Holding: The trust is not to be included in decedents gross estate At first glance it might seems that clause (2) of 2036(a) covers the present case, because the decedent for a period that did not in fact end before his death, retained the right in conjunction with another of the trustees, to designate the persons who should enjoy the trust property or the income therefrom However, we believe that the decedent effectively put that right beyond his own control or retention by imposing conditions upon the exercise of it This power was conditioned on the trustees determination that disbursement of the income was necessary to enable the beneficiary to whom it might be allocated to maintain himself and his family in comfort and in accordance with the station in life to which he belongs The event of the contingent power of the trustees to invade corpus or to disburse the net income of any year would remain the same as before his death or resignation Powers Retained by the Grantor Trustee One of the strings that grantors would most like to retain is the discretionary power to accumulate income or pay out the income and/or principal of the trust at such times, to such persons, and in such amounts as the grantor-trustee might determine Where the exercise of such a power is in the discretion of the grantor-trustee, either alone or in conjunction with other persons, the courts have 2036(a)(2) applicable Section 2036(a)(2) would be applicable to a trust in which D had retained the unrestricted right to change beneficiaries Section 2036(a)(2) also applies where D retained the power to distribute income currently to a sole beneficiary or instead to retain the income until the beneficiary reached 21 If a discretionary power over trust income and principal is effectively released by the grantor-trustee under state law more than 3 years before death, 2036(a)(2) is not applicable, but a gift tax would result As illustrated by Jennings, if an ascertainable standard limits the grantor-trustees discretionary power to distribute or accumulate income, the trust property will not be includible in his estate under 2036(a)(2) A standard is ascertainable if it is sufficiently definite such that a court with equitable powers will control the trustee to comply with the trust provision Examples include sickness, or changed circumstances, care, support, and medical attention, and for the support, education, and maintenance If the standard is not definite, a court of equity will not restrict the trustees discretion with the result that inclusion in the estate is required If the trustee has power to provide for the beneficiaries happiness or pleasure or use and benefit or reasonable requirements or best interest the standards is so loose that the trustees is in effect uncontrolled The Government has generally been unsuccessful in contending that the retention of broad administrative powers over trust property is sufficient to invoke 2036(a)(2) The courts have reasoned that administrative powers are exercisable as fiduciaries subject to the control of the courts This includes the power to retain or invest trust property Retention of Controls Over Trust by Grantor who is not Trustee If the grantor of a trust is not a trustee, but retains the power to remove the trustee and appoint himself as a successor trustee, the above rules are applied as if the grantor were a trustee Revenue Ruling 95-58 holds that a grantor has not retained trustee powers where the grantor has the power to remove a trustee and appoint a successor trustee that is not related or subordinate to the grantor within the meaning of 672(c) Ds retention of the power to direct trust investment after transferring assets in trust and designating another person as trustee does not trigger application of 2036 Miscellaneous Section 2036(a)(2) applies even if the transferors right to designate who shall possess or enjoy the property is subject to a contingency beyond her control which did not occur prior to her death The transferors ability to delay payment of income to a sole beneficiary will cause inclusion under 2036(a)(2) Regulation 20.2036-1(b)(3) states that the scope of 2036(a)(2) does not include a power over the transferred property itself which does not affect the enjoyment of the income received or earned during the decedents life Based on this regulation, Rev Rul 70-153 held that 2036(a)(2) did not apply to Ds power to terminate the trust and invest the corpus; however 2038 requires inclusion of the remainder interest Rev Rul 72-552 held that property transferred to a corporation was includible in Ds estate under 2036(a)(2) since Ds position as a member of the corporation gave him the power to designate the persons who would possess or enjoy the property Chapter 16 Transfers with Retained Powers to Alter, Amend, Revoke, or Terminate: 2038 Introduction (CB pg. 272) 2038 Revocable Transfers (a) In general - The value of the gross estate shall include the value of all property (1) Transfers after June 22, 1936 - To the extent of any interest therein of which the decedent has at any time made a transfer (except in case of a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth), by trust or otherwise, where the enjoyment thereof was subject at the date of his death to any change through the exercise of a power (in whatever capacity exercisable) by the decedent alone or by the decedent in conjunction with any other person (without regard to when or from what source the decedent acquired such power), to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate, or where any such power is relinquished during the 3 year period ending on the date of the decedent's death. (2) Transfers on or before June 22, 1936 - To the extent of any interest therein of which the decedent has at any time made a transfer (except in case of a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth), by trust or otherwise, where the enjoyment thereof was subject at the date of his death to any change through the exercise of a power, either by the decedent alone or in conjunction with any person, to alter, amend, or revoke, or where the decedent relinquished any such power during the 3 year period ending on the date of the decedent's death. Except in the case of transfers made after June 22, 1936, no interest of the decedent of which he has made a transfer shall be included in the gross estate under paragraph (1) unless it is includible under this paragraph. (b) Date of existence of power - For purposes of this section, the power to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate shall be considered to exist on the date of the decedent's death even though the exercise of the power is subject to a precedent giving of notice or even though the alteration, amendment, revocation, or termination takes effect only on the expiration of a stated period after the exercise of the power, whether or not on or before the date of the decedent's death notice has been given or the power has been exercised. In such cases proper adjustment shall be made representing the interests which would have been excluded from the power if the decedent had lived, and for such purpose, if the notice has not been given or the power has not been exercised on or before the date of his death, such notice shall be considered to have been given, or the power exercised, on the date of his death. Sec. 20.2038-1 Revocable transfers. (a) In general. A decedent's gross estate includes under section 2038 the value of any interest in property transferred by the decedent, whether in trust or otherwise, if the enjoyment of the interest was subject at the date of the decedent's death to any change through the exercise of a power by the decedent to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate, or if the decedent relinquished such a power in contemplation of death. However, section 2038 does not apply (1) To the extent that the transfer was for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth (see Sec. 20.2043-1); (2) If the decedent's power could be exercised only with the consent of all parties having an interest (vested or contingent) in the transferred property, and if the power adds nothing to the rights of the parties under local law; or (3) To a power held solely by a person other than the decedent. But, for example, if the decedent had the unrestricted power to remove or discharge a trustee at any time and appoint himself trustee, the decedent is considered as having the powers of the trustee. However, this result would not follow if he only had the power to appoint himself trustee under limited conditions which did not exist at the time of his death. (See last two sentences of paragraph (b) of this section.) Except as provided in this paragraph, it is immaterial in what capacity the power was exercisable by the decedent or by another person or persons in conjunction with the decedent; whether the power was exercisable alone or only in conjunction with another person or persons, whether or not having an adverse interest (unless the transfer was made before June 2, 1924; see paragraph (d) of this section); and at what time or from what source the decedent acquired his power (unless the transfer was made before June 23, 1936; see paragraph (c) of this section). Section 2038 is applicable to any power affecting the time or manner of enjoyment of property or its income, even though the identity of the beneficiary is not affected. For example, section 2038 is applicable to a power reserved by the grantor of a trust to accumulate income or distribute it to A, and to distribute corpus to A, even though the remainder is vested in A or his estate, and no other person has any beneficial interest in the trust. However, only the value of an interest in property subject to a power to which section 2038 applies is included in the decedent's gross estate under section 2038. (b) Date of existence of power. A power to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate will be considered to have existed at the date of the decedent's death even though the exercise of the power was subject to a precedent giving of notice or even though the alteration, amendment, revocation, or termination would have taken effect only on the expiration of a stated period after the exercise of the power, whether or not on or before the date of the decedent's death notice had been given or the power had been exercised. In determining the value of the gross estate in such cases, the full value of the property transferred subject to the power is discounted for the period required to elapse between the date of the decedent's death and the date upon which the alteration, amendment, revocation, or termination could take effect. In this connection, see especially Sec. 20.2031-7. However, section 2038 is not applicable to a power the exercise of which was subject to a contingency beyond the decedent's control which did not occur before his death (e.g., the death of another person during the decedent's life). See, however, section 2036(a)(2) for the inclusion of property in the decedent's gross estate on account of such a power. Section 2038 includes in the gross estate the value of all property transferred by the decedent where the enjoyment of the property was subject at the date of death to any change through Ds exercise of a power to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate The power to change the enjoyment of property through the exercise of powers specified in 2038(a)(1) is very similar to a power under 2036(a)(2), but the sections are neither coextensive nor mutually exclusive Lober v. United States Enjoyment Issue: Was there a retention of a benefit, so that 2038 would be applicable? Lober created trusts for his 5 sons, and he could accumulate and reinvest the income with the same freedom until his children reached 21 years of age Lober could hold the principal of each trust until the beneficiary reached 25, and at 21, they were to be paid the accumulated income A crucial term of the trust instruments was that Lober could at any time he saw fit turn all or any part of the principal of the trust over to his children Thus he could at will reduce the principal or pay it all to the beneficiaries, thereby terminating any trusteeship over it Holding: A donor who keeps so strong a hold over the actual and immediate enjoyment of what he puts beyond his own power to retake has not divested himself of that degree of control which 2038(a)(2) requires in order to avoid the tax. Inclusion. Section 2038(a)(2) requires inclusion in a decedents gross estate the value of all property that the decedent has previously transferred by trust, where the enjoyment thereof was subject at the date of his death to any change through the exercise of a power to alter, amend, or revoke. In Commissioner v. Holmes, we held that a power to terminate was the equivalent of a power to alter, amend, or revoke it, and we approved taxation on that basis We pointed out in the Holmes case the 2038(a)(2) was more concerned with present economic benefit than with technical vesting of title or estate And the Lober beneficiaries, like the Holmes beneficiaries, were granted no present right to immediate enjoyment of either income or principal To get this full enjoyment they had to wait until they reached the age of 25 unless their father sooner gave them the money and stocks by terminating the trust under the power of change he kept to the very date of his death Powers Included Under 2038 Discretionary Power to Distribute Income and Principal As Lober indicates, the grantors retention of the unrestricted power to distribute trust principal to the beneficiaries is equal to the retention of a power to terminate the trust under 2038 Rev Rul 70-513 held that the decedents retention of a veto power over the trustee whether to terminate or not required inclusion under 2038 Powers Limited by an Ascertainable Standard As in the case of 2036(a)(2), if Ds power to invade corpus is limited by an ascertainable standard (health, maintenance, education, or support) 2038 does not apply The theory of this exception in 2038, as in 2036, is that D does not have any power with an ascertainable standard, but only an obligation to carry out the terms of the transfer Power in Grantor to Remove Trustee and Appoint Self as Trustee Under Regulation 20.2038-1(a)(3), if D has the unrestricted power to remove or discharge a trustee and appoint herself trustee, she is considered as having the powers of the original trustee Thus, if the powers of the original trustee are not limited by an ascertainable standard, the power to remove the original trustee and appoint herself trustee requires the inclusion of the property in Ds gross estate under 2038 Conversely, if the power of the original trustee is limited by an ascertainable standard, the property is not included merely because D might have appointed herself as successor trustee Rev Rul 95-58 holds that a grantor has not retained powers over trust corpus or income because she has the power to remove a trustee and appoint a successor trustee that is not related or subordinated to her within the meaning of 672(c) Possession of Broad Administrative Powers In United States v. Powell the Commissioner asserted that the power in a grantor trustee to invest in life insurance or annuities gave him the power either to deprive the remaindermen of benefits The court held that this power did not require inclusion of the trust property under 2038 since it had to be exercised in a fiduciary capacity for the benefit of both income beneficiaries and remaindermen However, if a retained power to vote controlled corporation stock was involved in the above case, 2036(b) would require inclusion of the stock in the grantors gross estate Employee Death Benefits If D possess the power to change the beneficiary of a death benefit, 2038(a)(1) requires its inclusion Powers of Attorney Disputes often arise about the effects of gifts made on behalf of D by her representative pursuant to a power of attorney granted by D If such gifts are voidable under state law because the representative lacked authority to make gifts, 2038 will include them in Ds gross estate since D possessed the power to revoke the gift Contingent Powers and Powers Exercisable Only by Will As a condition precedent to taxability under 2038(a)(1) or (2), the power must exist at the date of Ds death Section 2038(b) provides that this requirement is satisfied even though the exercise of the power is subject to a precedent giving of notice or the transfer can take effect only upon the expiration of a stated period after the exercise of the power A negative inference from the language of 2038(b) would appear to be that the existence of more remotely contingent powers (i.e. those whose exercise depends upon the happening of an event beyond Ds control) at the time of Ds death would not be sufficient to bring the trust corpus within his taxable estate Regulation 20.2038-1(b) adopts this view and provides that 2038 is not applicable where the exercise of the power is subject to a contingency which did not occur before Ds death and is beyond Ds control, such as the death of another person during Ds lifetime This approach differs from that of 2036(a)(2), which includes contingent powers Joint Powers Sections 2038(a)(1) and (2) apply to powers held by the decedent either alone or in conjunction with any other person The Regulations interpret any other person to include powers shared jointly with one or more other person Regulation 20.2038-1(a)(2) applies to unanimous consent situations It provides that a 2038 power does not exist if Ds power can only be exercisable with the consent of all parties having an interest in the transferred property and such power adds nothing to the rights of the parties under local law Necessity of a Transfer by Decedent and the Reservation of the Power in that Transfer There must be a transfer Section 2038(a)(1) requires inclusion of property subject to a power to alter, amend, revoke or terminate without regard to when or from what source the decedent acquired such power This contrasts starkly with 2036(a) which applies only to powers actually retained by the transferor It is not necessary that the power to alter, amend, revoke, or terminate the enjoyment of the beneficial interests be retained by the donor at the time of the transfer The mere possession thereof by the donor at the time of death is the factor that results in the inclusion of the value of the transferred property in his gross estate The courts have not applied 2038, however, where the transferor did not create the powers that she subsequently acquires Amount Includable Section 2036 requires that all property subject to the retained power be included in the decedents gross estate, and in contrast, 2038 only includes in D's estate the property affected by the 2038 power When the two sections both apply to the same estate, the courts apply the section resulting in the largest inclusion A release of a 2038 power precludes the inclusion of the value of the property subject to the power under 2038 However, if the release occurs within three years of the transferors death, 2035(a) requires such inclusion There is the full and adequate consideration exception to consider as well Summary of Differences between Section 2036(a)(2) and Section 2038 (CB pg. 283) Section 2038 only includes in Ds gross estate the specific property interest subject to Ds power Section 2036(a)(2) only applies to powers that affect income interests, and in contrast, 2038 applies to powers over both income and corpus Section 2036 requires that D have retained the power at the time of the transfer, while 2038 does not have such a requirement, as it applies regardless of when or from what source decedent acquired such power Although 2036(a)(2) does not apply to the acceleration of a remainder interest, and 2038 does Unlike 2036, 2038 does not apply to powers subject to contingencies beyond Ds control See Chapter 16 Problems Chapter 17 Transfers with Retained Reversionary Interests: 2037 Introduction (CB pg. 286) Sec. 2037. Transfers taking effect at death (a) General rule - The value of the gross estate shall include the value of all property to the extent of any interest therein of which the decedent has at any time after September 7, 1916, made a transfer (except in case of a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth), by trust or otherwise, if (1) possession or enjoyment of the property can, through ownership of such interest, be obtained only by surviving the decedent, and (2) the decedent has retained a reversionary interest in the property (but in the case of a transfer made before October 8, 1949, only if such reversionary interest arose by the express terms of the instrument of transfer), and the value of such reversionary interest immediately before the death of the decedent exceeds 5 percent of the value of such property. (b) Special rules - For purposes of this section, the term \"reversionary interest\" includes a possibility that property transferred by the decedent (1) may return to him or his estate, or (2) may be subject to a power of disposition by him, but such term does not include a possibility that the income alone from such property may return to him or become subject to a power of disposition by him. The value of a reversionary interest immediately before the death of the decedent shall be determined (without regard to the fact of the decedent's death) by usual methods of valuation, including the use of tables of mortality and actuarial principles, under regulations prescribed by the Secretary. In determining the value of a possibility that property may be subject to a power of disposition by the decedent, such possibility shall be valued as if it were a possibility that such property may return to the decedent or his estate. Notwithstanding the foregoing, an interest so transferred shall not be included in the decedent's gross estate under this section if possession or enjoyment of the property could have been obtained by any beneficiary during the decedent's life through the exercise of a general power of appointment (as defined in section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-11-A-III-2041.html\" 2041) which in fact was exercisable immediately before the decedent's death. See Regulation 20.2037-1 Section 2037 requires the gross estate to include the value at the date of death of any interest in property that can be obtained only by surviving the decedent. Section 2037 applies where: D has transferred property; The possession or enjoyment of the property is conditioned upon surviving D; D has retained a reversionary interest that may bring the property to D or Ds estate, or becomes subject to her power of disposition; and The value of such reversionary interest immediately before her death exceeds 5% of the value of the transferred property. The second requirement means that the death of D is a precondition to possession by the transferee or the transferees estate In example 3, D irrevocably transfers property in trust, income to S, Ds spouse for life, and upon Ss death, corpus to D if living, and if not, to Ds child C or Cs estate If immediately prior to Ds death, the value of the possibility that the property would revert to D upon Ss death exceeds 5% of the value of the transferred property, 2037 will apply Ds death is a precondition to possession by C or Cs estate, and under 2037 the value of all interests in the property that are dependent on surviving D, here the value of Cs remainder interest, will be includable in Ds estate Operation of Section 2037 (CB pg. 287) Estate of Thacher v. Commissioner Divorce Issue: Whether respondent (IRS) erred in including in the taxable value of the corpus of the wifes trust under 2037, the value of the widows life interest In the trust created by decedent for his wife, the decedent retained a specific reversion by a provision that in case of divorce or legal separation or in case his wife predeceased him, the corpus of the trust was to be paid over to him by the trustee This trust further provided that in the case his wife survived him and remarried, the trust would terminate and the corpus paid over to the trusts created for decedents children Holding: The wifes life estate was a transfer intended to take effect in possession or enjoyment at or after the decedents death, within the meaning of 2037, and the value of such life estate is includible in the gross estate of the decedent Petitioners conceded that 2037 applies to the remainder interest of the wifes estate and are challenging the life interest The contingency based upon wifes death would cause the remainder interest to be included in decedents gross estate under 2037, but has no bearing upon the inclusion of the life estate It cannot be questioned that the wife became entitled to income from the trust estate immediately upon the transfer in question and that this was a positive, substantial, and present beneficial interest which could have been enforced against the trustee and could not have been cut off or diminished by actions of the decedent It likewise cannot be questioned, however, that by express terms of the trust instrument the decedent retained the right to have the entire corpus of the trust estate returned to him in the event they were divorced or legally separated This was a condition which affected the life estate of the wife and might terminate it at any time while the parties were alive, and only upon the death of the decedent was this possible termination of the wifes life estate removed Only then did the wifes interest in the income of the trust estate ripen into an absolute, unconditional life estate, subject only to termination in the event of remarriage The Transfer Requirement For the terms of 2037 to apply, there need not have been a direct transfer; it is sufficient that the decedent transferred something to a third party in exchange for the benefit The transfer requirement of 2037 should be satisfied in employee death benefit cases where the employees services have created the benefit and the employee has determined that his death will constitute the operative event on which the property will be delivered to the objects of his bounty The Survivorship Requirement The last sentence of 2037(b) now makes clear that the section is inapplicable if possession or enjoyment could have been obtained by any beneficiary during the decedents life through the exercise of a general power of appointment which in fact was exercisable immediately before the decedents death Reversionary Interest Section 2037(b)(1) defines reversionary interest to include the possibility that Ds transferred property may return to D or Ds estate In Thacher, D possessed a reversionary interest under 2037 because his wifes interest would revert to him if divorce occurred It was irrelevant that a divorce never occurred; the possibility of a divorce which would trigger the reversion was sufficient for 2037 to apply Section 2037(b)(2) expands the scope of the reversionary interest beyond the possibility of the property returning to D or Ds estate, as it includes property that is subject to a power of disposition In Estate of Tarver v. Commissioner, the court concluded that D had a reversionary interest in a trust he had created where the trust instrument stated that, if certain events did not occur during his life, it would terminate and its corpus go to whomever D designated as a residual legatee in his will The court stated: Since the decedent retained the power during his lifetime to change the provisions of his will relating to the disposition of the residuary estate, it is obvious that there was a possibility that the property transferred in trust might be subject to a power of disposition by him Valuation of the Reversionary Interest Section 7520(a)(1) stated that the values of reversionary interests shall be determined under actuarial tables prescribed by the IRS The value of a reversionary interest equals the product of the probability that D will survive the measuring life times the value of the interest D would have received if D had survived Amount Includable Once Ds reversionary interest has been valued for purposes of the 5% requirement, the amount includible in Ds estate must be determined If the 5% test is not satisfied, then the actual value of the reversionary interest should be included in Ds estate under 2033 unless the interest is terminated by Ds death But, if the 5% requirement is met, then the entire value of the interest subject to the reversion is included in Ds gross estate Section 2037(a)(1) states that 2037 does not apply to a transfer which is a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or moneys worth See Chapter 17 Problems Chapter 18 The Adequate and Full Consideration Exception for 2036-2038 The Exception (CB pg. 296) When a person transfers property in which she retains an interest or power, 2036-2038 do not include such value in her gross estate if the transfer was a bona fide sale for an adequate and full consideration in money or moneys worth Estate of DAmbrosio v. Commissioner Sale of remainder interest Issue: Whether the sale of a remainder interest for its FMV constitutes adequate and full consideration within the meaning of 2036(a) Decedent owned one half of the preferred stock of Vaparo, and these 470 shares had a FMV of $2,350,000 In 1987, at the age of 80, decedent transferred her remainder interest in her shares to Vaparo in exchange for an annuity which was to pay her $269,039 per year and retained her income interest in the shares According to the actuarial tables, the annuity has a FMV of $1,324,014 which the parties stipulate is also the FMV of the remainder interest Holding: Because we conclude that the tax court erred as a matter of law when it determined that the consideration received for the remainder interest was not adequate and full, we will reverse and remand for it to enter judgment in favor of the estate Under 2033, a decedents gross estate includes the value of all property to the extent of any interest therein of the decedent at the time of his death Section 2036(a) effectively discourages manipulative transfers of remainder interests which are really testamentary in character by pulling back the full fee simple value of the transferred property into the gross estate, except when the transfer was a bona fide sale for adequate and full consideration Allen, is inapposite, as the Commissioner now conceded, because it involved the sale of a life estate after the remainder had already been disposed of by gift, a testamentary transaction with a palpable tax evasion motive This case in contrast, involves the sale of a remainder interest for its stipulated FMV Nevertheless, we agree with its rationale that consideration should be measured against the value that would have been drawn into the gross estate absent the transfer Unless replaced by property of equal value that could be exposed to inclusion in the decedents gross estate, the property transferred in a testamentary transaction of the type described in the statute must be included in his gross estate Rather than evaluate the adequacy of the consideration at the time the decedents dies, we will compare the value of the remainder transferred to value of the consideration received, measured as of the date of transfer After parsing the language of 2036(a), we cannot agree with the Gradows courts conclusion that property refers to the fee simple interest and that adequate consideration must be measured against that value Rather, we believe that the clear import of the phrase to the extent of any interest therein is that the gross estate shall include the value of the remainder interest, unless it was sold for adequate and fair consideration We think it is likely that although the choice of verbiage was less than precise, the drafters meant merely to refer to the transferred property so as to distinguish it from other property owned by the estate It strains the judicial imagination, however, to conclude that the drafters used the term of art interest in property when they meant simply property The court was not concerned that this transaction was abusive, even though the decedent was 80 when the transferred occurred If the full, fee simple value of the property at the time of death is pulled back into the gross estate under 2036(a), subject only to an offset for the consideration received, then the post sale appreciation of the transferred asset will be taxed at death Indeed it will be double taxed because all things being would, the consideration she received will also have appreciated and will be subject to tax on its increased value In addition, it would appear virtually impossible, under the tax courts reasoning ever to sell a remainder interest, if the adequacy of the consideration must be measured against the fee simple value of the property at the time of the transfer, the transferor will have to find an arms length buyer willing to pay a fee simple price for a future interest Illustrative Materials (CB pg. 309) Subsequent Court Decisions In Wheeler v. United States, the court adopted the DAmbrosio holding and explained why Ds estate had not been depleted as follows: The sale of remainder interest for its actuarial value does not deplete the sellers estate The actuarial value of the remainder interest equals the amount that will grow to a principal sum equal to the value of the property that passes to the remainderman at termination of the retained interest The actuarial tables are premised on the recognition that at the end of the actuarial period, there is no discernable difference between an estate holder retaining the full fee interest in the estate and an estate holder retaining income from the life estate and selling the remainder interest for its actuarial value in either case, the estate is not depleted Estate Freeze Aspect of DAmbrosio Section 2702 significantly reduces the desirability of using the sale of remainder interests as an estate planning device Where D transfers a remainder interest to family members while retaining a life interest, 2702 treats the retained life interest as having zero value The result is that D is deemed to have made a taxable gift to the extent the value of her interest in the entire property exceeds the consideration received for the remainder interest Congress adopted 2702 because it was concerned that the actuarial and interest rate assumptions used to value remainder interests could be manipulated in the family context where the transferor and transferee might know that the assumptions underestimated the remainder interests actuarial value Partial Consideration and Section 2043(a) Section 2043(a) states that where a transferor receives consideration that does not qualify as adequate and full consideration, the transferors gross estate shall include only the excess of the FMV of the property at the time of death over the value of the consideration Courts have also suggested that adequate and full consideration need only be of approximately equal value, but this is a risky proposition to be testing See Chapter 18 Problems Chapter 19 Gift Tax Effects of Transfers with Retained Interests or Powers Introduction (CB pg. 313) The application of the estate tax to an inter vivos transfer because of a retained power does not necessarily relieve the inter vivos transfer of gift tax consequences It is possible for a transfer to be subject to a gift tax and subsequently to be included on the transferors gross income Two mechanisms exist for avoiding double taxation of the same transfer First, 2012 avoids double taxation of gifts made prior to 1977 which are included in the decedents gross estate by providing a credit for the gift taxes paid against the estate Second, the gifts made after 1976 which are included in the gross estate avoid double taxation due to the method of calculating the estate tax in 2001(b) Since adjusted taxable gifts do not include post 1976 gifts which are included in the decedents gross estate and since the tentative tax is reduced by the gift tax paid on all post 1976 gifts, the post 1976 gifts are not taxed twice Transfers with Retained Powers (CB pg. 314) Sec. 2501. Imposition of tax (a) Taxable transfers (1) General rule - A tax, computed as provided in section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-12-A-2502.html\" 2502, is hereby imposed for each calendar year on the transfer of property by gift during such calendar year by any individual resident or nonresident. (2) Transfers of intangible property - Except as provided in paragraph (3), paragraph (1) shall not apply to the transfer of intangible property by a nonresident not a citizen of the United States. (3) Exception (A) Certain individuals - Paragraph (2) shall not apply in the case of a donor who, within the 10-year period ending with the date of transfer, lost United States citizenship, unless such loss did not have for one of its principal purposes the avoidance of taxes under this subtitle or subtitle A. (B) Certain individuals treated as having tax avoidance purpose - For purposes of subparagraph (A), an individual shall be treated as having a principal purpose to avoid such taxes if such individual is so treated under section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-A-1-N-II-A-877.html\" \\l \"_a__2_\" 877(a)(2). (C) Exception for certain individuals - Subparagraph (B) shall not apply to a donor meeting the requirements of section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-A-1-N-II-A-877.html\" \\l \"_c__1_\" 877(c)(1). (D) Credit for foreign gift taxes - The tax imposed by this section solely by reason of this paragraph shall be credited with the amount of any gift tax actually paid to any foreign country in respect of any gift which is taxable under this section solely by reason of this paragraph. (E) Cross reference - For comparable treatment of long-term lawful permanent residents who ceased to be taxed as residents, see section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-A-1-N-II-A-877.html\" \\l \"_e_\" 877(e). (4) Burden of proof - If the Secretary establishes that it is reasonable to believe that an individual's loss of United States citizenship would, but for paragraph (3), result in a substantial reduction for the calendar year in the taxes on the transfer of property by gift, the burden of proving that such loss of citizenship did not have for one of its principal purposes the avoidance of taxes under this subtitle or subtitle A shall be on such individual. (5) Transfers to political organizations - Paragraph (1) shall not apply to the transfer of money or other property to a political organization (within the meaning of section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-A-1-F-VI-527.html\" \\l \"_e__1_\" 527(e)(1)) for the use of such organization. Sec. 2511. Transfers in general (a) Scope - Subject to the limitations contained in this chapter, the tax imposed by section HYPERLINK \"http://www.fourmilab.ch/uscode/26usc/www/t26-B-12-A-2501.html\" 2501 shall apply whether the transfer is in trust or otherwise, whether the gift is direct or indirect, and whether the property is real or personal, tangible or intangible; but in the case of a nonresident not a citizen of the United States, shall apply to a transfer only if the property is situated within the United States. Estate of Sanford v. Commissioner Reserving powers Issue: Whether in the case of an inter vivos transfer of property in trust, by a donor reserving to himself the power to designate new beneficiaries other than himself, the gift becomes complete and subject to the gift tax at the time of the relinquishment of the power In 1913, before the enactment of the gift tax, decedent created a trust of personal property for the benefit of named beneficiaries, reserving to himself the power to terminate the trust in whole or in part, or to modify it by changing the beneficiaries In 1919 he surrendered the power to revoke the trust by an appropriate writing in which he reserved the right to modify any and all of the trust but provided that this right shall in no way be deemed or construed to include any right or privilege in the donor to withdraw principal and income from any trust In 1924, after the effective date of the gift tax statute, decedent renounced his remaining power to modify the trust Holding: The gift was completed and taxable in 1924 There is nothing in the language of the statute, and our attention has not been directed to anything in its legislative history to suggest that Congress had any purpose to tax gifts before the donor had fully parted with his interest in the property given, or that the test of the completeness of the taxed gift was to be any different from that to be applied in determining whether the donor has retained an interest such that it becomes subject to the estate tax upon its relinquishment at death An important, if not the main purpose of the gift tax was to prevent or compensate for avoidance of death taxes by taxing the gifts of property inter vivos which, but for the gifts, would be subject in its original or converted form to the tax laid upon transfers at death Section 2012(a) provides that when a tax has been imposed by 2502 upon a gift, the value of which is required by any provision of the statute taxing the estate to be included in the gross estate, the gift tax is to be credited on the estate tax Section 2012 is without application unless there is a gift inter vivos which is taxable independently of any requirement that it shall be included in the gross estate The point was decided in the Guggenheim case where it was held that a gift upon trust, with power in the donor to revoke it is not taxable as a gift because the transfer is incomplete, and that the transfer whether inter vivos or at death becomes complete and taxable only when the power of control is relinquished A gift would not seem to be complete, for purposes of the tax where the donor has reserved the power to determine whether the donee ultimately entitled to receive and enjoy the property are of such a class as to exempt from the gift from taxation It is true, of course, that under it gift taxes will not be imposed on transactions which fall short of being completed gifts Camp v. Commissioner Completed gifts Issue: When was the transfer of property to a trust a completed gift for gift tax purposes? The trust instrument provided that the income shall be payable to taxpayers wife Alida during her life, and that upon her death the principal of the trust should be paid to the then living issue of the donor per stirpes; and in default of such issue, that the trustee should continue to hold the principal in trust, paying the income therefrom to Johnanna Bullock, mother of the donor, during her life, and upon her death, that the trustee should pay the principal of the trust fund to H. Ridgely Bullock, half brother of the donor, or if he be then dead, unto the then living issue of Ridgely per stirpes, or if there be none, to the trustees of Princeton In conjunction with Ridgely or Johnanna the donor could modify the trust in any manner or revoke in whole or in part, the indenture and the trusts then existing In 1934, the taxpayer in connection with Ridgely exercised the power to alter or amend by inserting a provision that Alida, should receive the income of the trust only so long as she, during the donors lifetime, should continue to be his wife In 1937, taxpayer reserved the power to alter, amend, or revoke the trust in sole conjunction with his wife In 1946, the taxpayer and his wife further modified the trust by striking out any provision to revoke, modify, or alter Holding: By the amendment of 1946, whereby all power to revoke, alter or modify the trust was eliminated, there resulted a taxable gift of the then value of the corpus, minus the sum determined to be the value of the gift to Alida on 1937, and minus also the values of any completed gifts which may be deemed to have been made at the time of the creation of the trust in 1932 The gift tax was not aimed at every transfer of the legal title without consideration, which would include a transfer to trustees to hold for the use of the grantor, but was aimed, rather, at transfers of the title that have the quality of a gift, and a gift is not consummated until put beyond recall Cases have elaborated upon this concept of a gift and have settled that a transfer in trust is incomplete as a gift, not only where the donor reserves the power to revest the trust property in himself, but also where he reserves the power to alter the disposition of the property or the income therefrom in some way not beneficial to himself As to any property, or part thereof or interest therein, of which the donor has so parted with dominion and control as to leave in him no power to change the disposition thereof, whether for his own benefit or for the benefit of another, the gift is complete But, if upon a transfer of property the donor reserves any power over the disposition thereof, the gift may be wholly incomplete, or may be partially incomplete, depending upon all the facts in the particular case The relinquishment or termination of a power to change the disposition of transferred property, occurring otherwise than by the death of the donor, is regarded as the event which completes the gifts and causes the tax to apply The following propositions are clear: If the trust instrument gives